{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_0","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'cat /Users/lchingen/hivemind/clean-arch/e2e-comparison-2026-11-13/full-eligibility/control-reader-brief.md /Users/lchingen/hivemind/clean-arch/e2e-comparison-2026-11-13/full-eligibility/controls/control-packet-6.json'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_0","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'cat /Users/lchingen/hivemind/clean-arch/e2e-comparison-2026-11-13/full-eligibility/control-reader-brief.md /Users/lchingen/hivemind/clean-arch/e2e-comparison-2026-11-13/full-eligibility/controls/control-packet-6.json'","aggregated_output":"# Stage B: reading the control theses (read-only)\n\nYou are an independent reader. Do not edit files, run model calls or start runs, and do not read any file other than this brief\nand the one packet you are given.\n\nEach item of your packet pairs a **ruler question** (a question written by hand for an earlier, frozen measurement, with its\nunit, due day and ladder of rungs) with a **judged question** the system wrote itself for a thesis it opened (its question, the\noutcome it looks at, due day, year and rungs). It also lists **reader B's decisive items**: the evidence an earlier reader, called\nreader B, cited for the frozen answer to the ruler question (each with its day, title, passage and meaning), and **the evidence\nshown to the judge**: every evidence entry the system's judge was shown for the judged question in any of its requests on or\nbefore 2026-07-14 (empty when there is none). You are not told either answer, and you do not judge which answer is right.\n\nFor each item, answer three questions.\n\n1. `same_question`: **yes** when the judged question asks what the ruler question asks: the same quantity measured the same way\n   (the same total, scope and unit: not a part of it, a ceiling on it, another quantity or another unit), or the same event,\n   for the same period. **no** otherwise. Wording may differ; a judged question that asks for more or less than the ruler's,\n   or for another period, is **no**.\n2. `same_due`: for an **event** (a yes-or-no question), **yes** only when the judged question's deadline is the ruler's\n   deadline. For a **figure**, **yes** when the two due days both mark the publication of the same period's figure (for\n   example, both the annual report of the same fiscal year, even if the two days differ); **no** otherwise.\n3. `items`: for **each** of reader B's decisive items, by its id, **shown** when its content is present in the evidence shown to\n   the judge (the same fact or figure, in any wording, from any source), **not_shown** when it is absent. Search the entries for\n   the figure or the fact; do not mark an item shown because a related item is.\n\nReturn one JSON object per item, one per line, in packet order, and nothing else (no prose, no code fence):\n`{\"control\": 3, \"same_question\": \"yes\"|\"no\", \"same_due\": \"yes\"|\"no\", \"items\": {\"i005\": \"shown\"|\"not_shown\", ...}, \"why\": \"one sentence naming what decides each no and each not_shown\"}`.\n\n## Third reader (only where the two readers differ)\n\nA third reader of each family is given this brief, the packet, and a list of the disputed points, each a control number and a\nfield (`same_question`, `same_due`, or `item <id>`), without the two readers' answers. For each point, answer that one question\nafresh by the rules above and return one JSON object per point, one per line, and nothing else:\n`{\"control\": 3, \"field\": \"item i005\", \"ruling\": \"yes\"|\"no\"|\"shown\"|\"not_shown\", \"why\": \"one sentence\"}` (`yes`/`no` for the two\nquestion fields, `shown`/`not_shown` for an item).\n[\n {\n  \"control\": 21,\n  \"ruler_question\": {\n   \"question\": \"Will Nvidia begin shipping its Rubin generation by December 31, 2026?\",\n   \"unit\": \"yes/no\",\n   \"due_day\": \"2026-12-31\",\n   \"rungs\": [\n    {\n     \"label\": \"Yes\",\n     \"condition\": \"Nvidia has begun shipping Rubin by December 31, 2026.\",\n     \"lo\": null,\n     \"hi\": null\n    },\n    {\n     \"label\": \"No\",\n     \"condition\": \"Nvidia has not begun shipping Rubin by December 31, 2026.\",\n     \"lo\": null,\n     \"hi\": null\n    }\n   ]\n  },\n  \"judged_question\": {\n   \"view_id\": \"v3\",\n   \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA confirm that first shipments of its Vera Rubin AI chip platform to customers have begun by December 31, 2026, through a press release, earnings call, or quarterly filing?\",\n   \"looks_at\": \"First shipments of Vera Rubin to customers, confirmed by NVIDIA.\",\n   \"due_day\": \"2026-12-31\",\n   \"year\": \"2026\",\n   \"kind\": \"asset\"\n  },\n  \"judged_rungs\": [\n   {\n    \"position\": 1,\n    \"label\": \"Yes\",\n    \"condition\": \"NVIDIA confirms that first shipments of Vera Rubin to customers have begun by December 31, 2026.\",\n    \"lo\": null,\n    \"hi\": null\n   },\n   {\n    \"position\": 2,\n    \"label\": \"Ruled out\",\n    \"condition\": \"NVIDIA formally announces after July 2, 2026, that it has canceled or abandoned the Vera Rubin platform before any first shipment to a customer. This rung holds when that, or any other decision against the event of rung 1 as a whole, or a formal abandonment or withdrawal of it as a whole, was made after 2026-07-02 and stands on 2026-12-31, and only when rung 1 does not.\",\n    \"lo\": null,\n    \"hi\": null\n   },\n   {\n    \"position\": 3,\n    \"label\": \"Still open\",\n    \"condition\": \"Neither rung 1 nor rung 2 holds on 2026-12-31.\",\n    \"lo\": null,\n    \"hi\": null\n   }\n  ],\n  \"reader_b_decisive_items\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"i105\",\n    \"day\": \"2026-07-05\",\n    \"title\": \"Nvidia's next-gen AI rack system delayed to 2028 on manufacturing snags, SemiAnalysis says\",\n    \"passage\": \"Nvidia's current-generation Rubin systems are in full production and begin shipping this fall to eight cloud partners\",\n    \"meaning\": {\n     \"who\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"what\": \"Vera Rubin systems in full production; shipping to eight cloud partners including AWS, Microsoft Azure and Google Cloud\",\n     \"period\": \"this fall (2026)\",\n     \"status\": \"happened (full production); plan (shipping this fall)\"\n    }\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"i219\",\n    \"day\": \"2026-07-06\",\n    \"title\": \"Nvidia Vera Rubin rack costs $7.8M — memory is why\",\n    \"passage\": \"First shipments are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth.\",\n    \"meaning\": {\n     \"what\": \"Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 first shipments and volume ramp\",\n     \"period\": \"first shipments Q3 2026; volume ramp Q4 2026\",\n     \"status\": \"plan\"\n    }\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"i202\",\n    \"day\": \"2026-07-06\",\n    \"title\": \"Nvidia's Kyber rack for Rubin Ultra reportedly delayed to 2028, stopgap solution also axed due to customer pushback — Analyst firm SemiAnalysis says PCB midplane problems led to the delay [Updated]\",\n    \"passage\": \"Nvidia's 2026 Rubin GPUs, which reuse the current Oberon rack, aren't part of the reported delay.\",\n    \"meaning\": {\n     \"who\": \"Tom's Hardware\",\n     \"what\": \"scope of the reported delay: Rubin Ultra/Kyber only; 2026 Rubin on Oberon unaffected\",\n     \"period\": \"2026\",\n     \"status\": \"claim\"\n    }\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"i207\",\n    \"day\": \"2026-07-06\",\n    \"title\": \"Nvidia Delays Next-Generation Kyber Server Architecture as Manufacturing Hurdles Give Rivals a Rare Opening\",\n    \"passage\": \"the company’s current-generation Rubin systems are already in full production and set to start shipping this fall to eight major cloud partners, including Amazon Web Services, Microsoft Azure, and Google Cloud\",\n    \"meaning\": {\n     \"who\": \"NVIDIA (as restated)\",\n     \"what\": \"Rubin systems in full production; first shipments to eight cloud partners incl. AWS, Azure, Google Cloud\",\n     \"period\": \"this fall\",\n     \"status\": \"happened\",\n     \"status_note\": \"in full production; shipping this fall is a plan\"\n    }\n   }\n  ],\n  \"evidence_shown_to_the_judge_through_2026_07_14\": [\n   \"- e40 [fact; Justin Pope / Fool.com; original source NVIDIA 2026-07-02; from gnews:af6e4910e877d5682a773d4b 2026-07-02] In an article dated July 2, 2026, Fool.com reported that NVIDIA's next-generation AI chip platform, Vera Rubin, is in full production and could begin shipping later in 2026.\\n    Quote: Vera Rubin, Nvidia's next-generation AI chip platform, is in full production and could begin shipping later this year.\",\n   \"- e41 [party_statement; Justin Pope / Fool.com; original source Jensen Huang / NVIDIA ; from gnews:af6e4910e877d5682a773d4b 2026-07-02] NVIDIA CEO Jensen Huang has stated expectations for $1 trillion in orders for NVIDIA's Vera Rubin and Grace Blackwell chips through 2027, according to Fool.com.\\n    Quote: CEO Jensen Huang has laid out expectations for $1 trillion in orders for Vera Rubin and Grace Blackwell chips through 2027.\",\n   \"- e41 [party_statement; Justin Pope / Fool.com; original source Jensen Huang / NVIDIA ; from gnews:af6e4910e877d5682a773d4b 2026-07-02] NVIDIA CEO Jensen Huang has stated expectations for $1 trillion in orders for NVIDIA's Vera Rubin and Grace Blackwell chips through 2027, according to Fool.com.\\n    Quote: CEO Jensen Huang has laid out expectations for $1 trillion in orders for Vera Rubin and Grace Blackwell chips through 2027.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e72; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e72 [party_statement; Bram Berkowitz, Fool.com; original source NVIDIA (CEO Jensen Huang) ; from gnews:e4e5f1b5c0c68381e4c486e1 2026-07-03] NVIDIA CEO Jensen Huang said in March 2026 that he expects the company's Blackwell and Vera Rubin chips to generate $1 trillion in sales between that time and the end of 2027, as reported by Fool.com.\\n    Quote: In March, CEO Jensen Huang said he expects the company's Blackwell and Vera Rubin chips to generate $1 trillion in sales between that time and the end of 2027.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e41; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e81 [party_statement; Josh Parker, head of corporate sustainability at NVIDIA (via bgr.com article by Jonathan Sayers); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:a8fab49de53d125314a6688a 2026-07-04] NVIDIA, in a company blog post by Josh Parker, head of corporate sustainability, announced that NVIDIA DSX AI factories are hosting NVIDIA's Rubin AI infrastructure with no fans or cold aisles, using a 100% liquid-cooling design in which coolant enters the chip at 45°C and exits at 55°C.\\n    Quote: In Nvidia's blog post by Josh Parker, head of corporate sustainability at Nvidia, it was announced that Nvidia DSX AI factories are hosting Nvidia's Rubin AI infrastructure with no fans or cold aisles. … Liquid coolant enters the chip at 45 degrees Celsius, absorbs heat from across the chip's surface, and exits at 55 degrees.\",\n   \"- e40 [fact; Justin Pope / Fool.com; original source NVIDIA 2026-07-02; from gnews:af6e4910e877d5682a773d4b 2026-07-02] In an article dated July 2, 2026, Fool.com reported that NVIDIA's next-generation AI chip platform, Vera Rubin, is in full production and could begin shipping later in 2026.\\n    Quote: Vera Rubin, Nvidia's next-generation AI chip platform, is in full production and could begin shipping later this year.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e93; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e93 [party_statement; CNBC; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:552f8c2bdfd080555d592587 2026-07-05] NVIDIA's current-generation Rubin systems are in full production and are scheduled to begin shipping in fall 2026 to eight cloud partners, including Amazon Web Services, Microsoft Azure and Google Cloud, according to CNBC.\\n    Quote: Nvidia's current-generation Rubin systems are in full production and begin shipping this fall to eight cloud partners, including Amazon Web Services, Microsoft Azure and Google Cloud.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e40; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e97 [fact; Adria Cimino, fool.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:2f953a56cd06571ab0afc348 2026-07-05] Nvidia is launching its first-ever stand-alone CPU this fall as part of the Vera Rubin platform, per the article's account of Nvidia's plan.\\n    Quote: In fact, it's launching its first-ever stand-alone CPU this fall as part of the Vera Rubin platform.\",\n   \"- e99 [party_statement; Adria Cimino, fool.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:2f953a56cd06571ab0afc348 2026-07-05] Nvidia forecasts $20 billion in stand-alone CPU sales this year.\\n    Quote: Nvidia already forecasts $20 billion in stand-alone CPU sales this year.\",\n   \"- e40 [fact; Justin Pope / Fool.com; original source NVIDIA 2026-07-02; from gnews:af6e4910e877d5682a773d4b 2026-07-02] In an article dated July 2, 2026, Fool.com reported that NVIDIA's next-generation AI chip platform, Vera Rubin, is in full production and could begin shipping later in 2026.\\n    Quote: Vera Rubin, Nvidia's next-generation AI chip platform, is in full production and could begin shipping later this year.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e93, e172; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e119 [third_party_view; MarketScale; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:f66e1689ec2e27e7eab2a2dd 2026-07-06] NVIDIA's Vera Rubin racks are reported to have first shipments scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth quarter, according to the article's account of the launch schedule.\\n    Quote: First shipments are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth.\",\n   \"- e120 [third_party_view; MarketScale; original source  ; from gnews:f66e1689ec2e27e7eab2a2dd 2026-07-06] The article reports that constrained supply of DRAM, NAND and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand is raising component costs across the market; contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, and device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into their upcoming products.\\n    Quote: Only a handful of companies buy racks at this scale. But the force behind the price, constrained supply of DRAM, NAND, and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand, is the same one now raising component costs across the market. Contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, and device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products. The AI build\",\n   \"- e134 [rumour; 247wallst.com (Danielle Liverance); original source  ; from gnews:3df0bbc8aff2c1d008bc0a32 2026-07-06] NVIDIA shares were reported to be under pressure on reports of delays across its upcoming lineup of Vera Rubin server systems; the reports are unattributed in the article.\\n    Quote: NVIDIA shares are under pressure on reports of delays across their upcoming lineup of Vera Rubin server systems.\",\n   \"- e168 [party_statement; NVIDIA spokesperson, via Investing.com (uk.finance.yahoo.com); original source NVIDIA Corporation ; from gnews:d5c48abb470f394f86549b6b 2026-07-06] An Nvidia spokesperson, in a brief statement emailed to Investing.com, refuted SemiAnalysis's claims that its next-generation rack architectures are delayed, saying: \\\"Our roadmap is intact.\\\"\\n    Quote: An Nvidia spokesperson swiftly refuted such claims in a brief statement emailed to Investing.com, stating: \\\"Our roadmap is intact.\\\"\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e92; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read it as a later version of e166 (); both are in force: SemiAnalysis reported that Nvidia's key interconnect technology CPO-NVSwitch, which links many chips into a single large system, will not arrive until the generation after next, called Feynman, meaning Nvidia lacks a proven way to scale Rubin Ultra to very large systems for now; the report says the gap could give competitors like AMD's MI500X or Google's TPUv8i Broadfly room to move in, and Nvidia plans to make up for the shortfall by selling more Oberon-Rubin racks in the existing form factor.\",\n   \"- e170 [party_statement; Mizuho analyst Jordan Klein, reported by Investing.com (uk.finance.yahoo.com); original source Hon Hai Precision Industry Co Ltd (management) ; from gnews:d5c48abb470f394f86549b6b 2026-07-06] Hon Hai Precision Industry, one of Nvidia's largest suppliers, reported strong earnings on the Friday before the 2026-07-06 article and its management forecast a continued acceleration in AI demand; Mizuho analyst Jordan Klein called this \\\"a very good sign into the NVDA launch of their new Vera Rubin rack.\\\" No figures for the results were given.\\n    Quote: The analyst additionally pointed to strong earnings last Friday from Hon Hai Precision Industry Co Ltd (TW:2317), one of Nvidia's largest suppliers. Klein noted that Hon Hai management forecasted a continued acceleration in AI demand, which the analyst said was \\\"a very good sign into the NVDA launch of their new Vera Rubin rack.\\\"\",\n   \"- e172 [party_statement; tikr.com (TIKR); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:d4c5dc540392f8df39aee250 2026-07-06] Nvidia's current-generation Rubin systems are in full production and are set to start shipping in fall 2026 to eight major cloud partners, including Amazon Web Services, Microsoft Azure, and Google Cloud, according to the article.\\n    Quote: That’s likely because the company’s current-generation Rubin systems are already in full production and set to start shipping this fall to eight major cloud partners, including Amazon Web Services, Microsoft Azure, and Google Cloud.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e40; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e180 [party_statement; Tom's Hardware (Luke James); original source NVIDIA 2026-07-06; from gnews:c75f16a6d64cc27fc28fb571 2026-07-06] An NVIDIA representative told Tom's Hardware, in a statement dated 7/6/2026, that \\\"Our roadmap is intact,\\\" providing no further details in response to follow-up questions about the reported Kyber delay.\\n    Quote: Update 7/6/2026 4:10pm PT : An Nvidia representative responded with a short statement to Tom's Hardware : \\\"Our roadmap is intact.\\\" Nvidia provided no further details in response to our follow-up questions.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e92; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e182 [third_party_view; Tom's Hardware (Luke James); original source  ; from gnews:c75f16a6d64cc27fc28fb571 2026-07-06] Tom's Hardware reported that the delay applies only to the Rubin Ultra phase and its Kyber rack; NVIDIA's 2026 Rubin GPUs, which reuse the current Oberon rack, are not part of the reported delay.\\n    Quote: The delay only applies only to the Rubin Ultra phase and its Kyber rack. Nvidia's 2026 Rubin GPUs, which reuse the current Oberon rack, aren't part of the reported delay.\",\n   \"- e195 [party_statement; CNBC; original source NVIDIA 2026-07-06; from gnews:b48593184704bbbe6eddbc03 2026-07-07] Nvidia disputed a SemiAnalysis report that its next-gen Kyber server rack was running into delays, according to CNBC; the item reports the dispute occurred on the Monday before the 2026-07-07 article and does not quote Nvidia's statement or give any revised schedule. Nvidia's denial is the company's own assertion that its product plan/schedule stands.\\n    Quote: Similar action took place in Nvidia Monday after the company disputed a report by SemiAnalysis that its next-gen Kyber server rack was running into delays.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e92; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read it as a later version of e182 (); both are in force: Tom's Hardware reported that the delay applies only to the Rubin Ultra phase and its Kyber rack; NVIDIA's 2026 Rubin GPUs, which reuse the current Oberon rack, are not part of the reported delay.\",\n   \"- e237 [party_statement; The Straits Times; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:c8c39f971721fd820aab5c1c 2026-07-08] Nvidia is working to release its next-generation Rubin AI chip line-up in the second half of 2026, following the Blackwell generation introduced in late 2024, according to the article.\\n    Quote: Nvidia is now working to release an even more powerful line-up called Rubin in the second half of 2026 .\",\n   \"- e284 [third_party_view; Bruno Ferreira, Tom's Hardware (citing Phoronix); original source Phoronix ; from gnews:9fbd0f954b9cc2281da531d1 2026-07-08] Phoronix test-drove one of Nvidia's 'upcoming' Arm-based Vera CPUs and reported strong single-threaded performance, favorably comparing it with Intel Xeon and AMD Epyc x86 processors in specific single-threaded workloads. The chip is described as upcoming, indicating it was in pre-launch testing rather than shipping.\\n    Quote: Only a little while back, Phoronix got the chance to test-drive one of Nvidia's upcoming Arm-based Vera CPUs. In certain approved workloads, the chip put up an impressive showing , nipping at the heels of its Xeon and Epyc x86 competitors. In specific single-threaded scenarios, Vera \\\"absolutely dusted the competition\\\" (our words).\",\n   \"- e41 [party_statement; Justin Pope / Fool.com; original source Jensen Huang / NVIDIA ; from gnews:af6e4910e877d5682a773d4b 2026-07-02] NVIDIA CEO Jensen Huang has stated expectations for $1 trillion in orders for NVIDIA's Vera Rubin and Grace Blackwell chips through 2027, according to Fool.com.\\n    Quote: CEO Jensen Huang has laid out expectations for $1 trillion in orders for Vera Rubin and Grace Blackwell chips through 2027.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e72, e312; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e306 [party_statement; barchart.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:57de52b4f33b0c903d256f9e 2026-07-09] Nvidia denied speculation that its Kyber rack-scale AI infrastructure is delayed, maintaining that its roadmap remains on track; the article states Kyber, designed for the Rubin Ultra platform, is expected to launch in 2027 as an integrated AI infrastructure system.\\n    Quote: However, the company denied the speculation, maintaining that its roadmap remains on track. Designed for the Rubin Ultra platform, Kyber is expected to launch in 2027 as an integrated AI infrastructure system and a key component of Nvidia's long-term AI infrastructure strategy.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e339; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read it as a later version of e134 (); both are in force: NVIDIA shares were reported to be under pressure on reports of delays across its upcoming lineup of Vera Rubin server systems; the reports are unattributed in the article.\",\n   \"- e312 [party_statement; Adam Spatacco, Fool.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:931ee0196ca609e637235fad 2026-07-09] NVIDIA management has articulated roughly $1 trillion in revenue visibility for its Blackwell and Vera Rubin processors across 2026 and 2027, anchored by multiyear commitments from hyperscalers and large enterprise customers, as reported by Fool.com on July 9, 2026.\\n    Quote: Management has also articulated roughly $1 trillion in revenue visibility for its Blackwell and Vera Rubin processors across 2026 and 2027, anchored by multiyear commitments from hyperscalers and large enterprise customers.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e41; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e339 [party_statement; Bloomberg (via moomoo); original source Nvidia ; from gnews:7b39859113d4e2b8ebd8aa86 2026-07-09] Nvidia issued a statement to Bloomberg denying claims of a technical delay and confirming that its product roadmap remains intact, a clarification the article says counters concerns that infrastructure bottlenecks could disrupt its AI competitive lead.\\n    Quote: NVIDIA recently issued a statement to Bloomberg denying claims of a technical delay, confirming that its product roadmap remains intact. This clarification serves as a counterweight to concerns that infrastructure bottlenecks could disrupt the company’s competitive lead in the artificial intelligence sector.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e306; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read it as a later version of e251 (); both are in force: Research firm SemiAnalysis stated in an X post that NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack system has hit manufacturing setbacks; the article says this could push NVIDIA to use its new credit/revenue-sharing model to sustain demand.\",\n   \"- e99 [party_statement; Adria Cimino, fool.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:2f953a56cd06571ab0afc348 2026-07-05] Nvidia forecasts $20 billion in stand-alone CPU sales this year.\\n    Quote: Nvidia already forecasts $20 billion in stand-alone CPU sales this year.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e382; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e365 [fact; quantumzeitgeist.com; original source NVIDIA 2026-07-07; from gnews:b666d35e5247a8ddcc2d7c72 2026-07-10] NVIDIA unveiled Vera, a new data center CPU architected for agentic AI workloads that prioritizes single-threaded (per-core) performance rather than the industry's trend toward higher core counts at the expense of per-core speed. This is a product unveiling, not a confirmed shipment or revenue event.\\n    Quote: NVIDIA is challenging a core tenet of modern data center design with Vera, a new CPU built to maximize single-threaded performance at scale, and AI innovator Perplexity is among the first to adopt it. … The newly unveiled NVIDIA Vera CPU is specifically architected to address the demands of agentic AI, a field where maximizing single-threaded performance is paramount\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e386; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e366 [fact; quantumzeitgeist.com; original source Perplexity 2026-07-07; from gnews:b666d35e5247a8ddcc2d7c72 2026-07-10] Perplexity, an AI company developing agentic systems, is among the first to adopt NVIDIA's Vera CPU; after testing it on real coding workflows, Perplexity is planning to integrate Vera into its upcoming production systems.\\n    Quote: Perplexity, a company actively developing agentic systems, is among the first to adopt the NVIDIA Vera CPU, signaling a significant endorsement of the new architecture’s capabilities. … Perplexity is now planning to integrate Vera into its upcoming production systems, demonstrating confidence in its ability to enhance agent performance and overall system efficiency.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e188; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e306 [party_statement; barchart.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:57de52b4f33b0c903d256f9e 2026-07-09] Nvidia denied speculation that its Kyber rack-scale AI infrastructure is delayed, maintaining that its roadmap remains on track; the article states Kyber, designed for the Rubin Ultra platform, is expected to launch in 2027 as an integrated AI infrastructure system.\\n    Quote: However, the company denied the speculation, maintaining that its roadmap remains on track. Designed for the Rubin Ultra platform, Kyber is expected to launch in 2027 as an integrated AI infrastructure system and a key component of Nvidia's long-term AI infrastructure strategy.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e339, e389; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read it as a later version of e134 (); both are in force: NVIDIA shares were reported to be under pressure on reports of delays across its upcoming lineup of Vera Rubin server systems; the reports are unattributed in the article.\",\n   \"- e407 [fact; I/O Fund (io-fund.com article); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:2dcfaef1a91c2976a4e347da 2026-07-12] NVIDIA has developed its CMX Context Memory Storage Platform, an SSD-based enclosure connected to Rubin GPUs and Vera CPUs over Spectrum-X Ethernet, using 64 BlueField-4 DPUs per rack connected to roughly 9,600 TB of SSD storage capacity; NVIDIA also created the STX reference architecture, a standardized blueprint for storage vendors to build CMX systems that connect to Vera Rubin racks. The I/O Fund reports this as an NVIDIA platform and potential new revenue driver.\\n    Quote: To solve for KV cache bottlenecks, Nvidia has developed its CMX Context Memory Storage Platform. CMX uses an SSD-based enclosure that is connected to Rubin GPUs and Vera CPUs over Spectrum-X Ethernet. … The hardware offload engine in CMX is the BlueField-4 DPUs. CMX incorporates 64 BlueField-4 DPUs, which connect to approximately 9,600 TB of SSD storage capacity per rack. … Nvidia also created the\",\n   \"- e412 [fact; barchart.com; original source NVIDIA (Jensen Huang) ; from gnews:82472d26336e1982cf8a3eff 2026-07-12] At Nvidia's latest product presentation, CEO Jensen Huang introduced the Vera CPU, a processor designed specifically for AI workloads and agentic AI systems; management says Vera works alongside Nvidia's AI chips and delivers roughly 1.8 times the performance of comparable x86 processors.\\n    Quote: During the company's latest product presentation, CEO Jensen Huang introduced the Vera CPU, a processor designed specifically for AI workloads and agentic AI systems. … Management says Vera delivers roughly 1.8 times the performance of comparable x86 processors while working alongside Nvidia's AI chips.\",\n   \"- e413 [rumour; barchart.com (citing unnamed reports); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:82472d26336e1982cf8a3eff 2026-07-12] According to unnamed reports, Nvidia has begun offering its new Vera processors to cloud customers, including some in China where regulations allow. The item frames this as 'reports suggest,' not a confirmed shipment or order.\\n    Quote: Reports also suggest Nvidia has begun offering the new processors to cloud customers, including some in China where regulations allow.\",\n   \"- e428 [fact; barchart.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:ededac685b09f7a9c6952202 2026-07-12] Nvidia recently launched its Vera Rubin AI platform (reported among its recent product and capability expansions); the item describes it as a launch, not as confirmed customer shipments.\\n    Quote: Nvidia recently launched its Vera Rubin AI platform, released the AI PC platform RTX Spark, expanded partnerships with companies such as Alphabet (GOOGL) and Marvell (MRVL), and further invested in networking, CPUs, and AI infrastructure.\",\n   \"- e429 [rumour; en.sedaily.com, citing unnamed semiconductor-industry sources; original source LG Electronics 2026-07-12; from gnews:bf96def37a9c3a5f19f5d43c 2026-07-12] LG Electronics' Production Research Institute (PRI) completed development in the first half of 2026 of an AI server-rack prototype built to the compute-tray specifications of Nvidia's \\\"Vera Rubin\\\" platform, with the R&D done at a 1.8-megawatt data center cluster at its Pyeongtaek site; LG plans reliability evaluations in the second half and then to secure orders from global big tech companies and begin mass production next year (2027). The report is attributed to unnamed semiconductor-industry sources; it does not state any order, volume or revenue.\\n    Quote: LG Electronics (066570.KS) is developing an AI chip-based server rack targeting Nvidia and plans to begin mass production next year. … According to the semiconductor industry on the 12th, LG Electronics' Production Research Institute (PRI) completed development of an AI server rack prototype matching the compute tray specifications of Nvidia's \\\"Vera Rubin\\\" in the first half of the year. LG plans t\",\n   \"- e434 [party_statement; gurufocus.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:dedacecd6d2ed5192c307199 2026-07-13] In investor-relations discussions, NVIDIA said development of its next-generation Rubin Ultra platform remains on schedule and confirmed there have been no changes to its NVLink architecture.\\n    Quote: The company said development of its next-generation Rubin Ultra platform remains on schedule and confirmed there have been no changes to its NVLink architecture.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read it as a later version of e134 (); both are in force: NVIDIA shares were reported to be under pressure on reports of delays across its upcoming lineup of Vera Rubin server systems; the reports are unattributed in the article.\",\n   \"- e237 [party_statement; The Straits Times; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:c8c39f971721fd820aab5c1c 2026-07-08] Nvidia is working to release its next-generation Rubin AI chip line-up in the second half of 2026, following the Blackwell generation introduced in late 2024, according to the article.\\n    Quote: Nvidia is now working to release an even more powerful line-up called Rubin in the second half of 2026 .\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e534; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e486 [fact; Yahoo Finance (finance.yahoo.com); original source SK Hynix ; from gnews:c73b90b63a733c6ab712fcdd 2026-07-14] According to a report about 21 days before the 2026-07-14 article, South Korea's SK Hynix is deliberately slowing its HBM4 ramp to redirect capacity into conventional DRAM, where shortages have pushed operating margins above those on HBM; the article characterizes this as a margin story rather than a demand story.\\n    Quote: The previous big move we wrote about was 21 days ago when the stock dropped 3.7% after a report that South Korea's SK Hynix is slowing its high-bandwidth memory (HBM) expansion rattled the AI-chip complex. … The headline sounds bearish for AI, but the underlying report is a margin story, not a demand story. SK Hynix is deliberately slowing its HBM4 ramp to redirect capacity into conventional DRAM,\",\n   \"- e506 [third_party_view; Alex Sirois, 247wallst.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:ac8a489e54fbb1b85a1c7a2c 2026-07-14] The article states that NVIDIA's Blackwell 300 is ramping and that the Vera Rubin platform is queued behind it, giving no shipping date for Vera Rubin. It is the author's description of the product roadmap rather than a new NVIDIA announcement.\\n    Quote: Blackwell 300 is ramping, and the Vera Rubin platform is queued behind it.\",\n   \"- e534 [party_statement; EBC (ebc.com); original source NVIDIA Corp. ; from gnews:f83ae0a4f01c18d48f20a629 2026-07-14] Nvidia's Blackwell Ultra chips are ramping up fast and the Vera Rubin architecture remains officially on track for late 2026, per EBC's report; an analyst cited in the article expects the August earnings report to be a catalyst on margins.\\n    Quote: Blackwell Ultra and Rubin: Target shipping dates for Blackwell Ultra chips are ramping up fast and the Vera Rubin architecture remains officially on track for late 2026, but the market is currently priced for absolute perfection.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e237; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e541 [fact; Jakarta Globe; original source Firmus Technologies ; from gnews:e001119c6342373f6b1f3cf4 2026-07-14] Under an eight-year strategic partnership between Firmus Technologies and Nvidia aimed at expanding AI infrastructure across the region, Firmus plans to deploy about 170,000 of Nvidia's latest-generation GPUs in phases from the first quarter of 2027 through early 2028, providing AI computing capacity for startups and fast-growing AI companies. The plan dates the customer deployment, not confirmed Nvidia shipments or revenue.\\n    Quote: The Batam campus is also part of an eight-year strategic partnership between Firmus Technologies and Nvidia aimed at expanding AI infrastructure across the region. … Under the partnership, Firmus plans to deploy about 170,000 of Nvidia's latest-generation graphics processing units (GPUs) in phases from the first quarter of 2027 through early 2028, providing AI computing capacity for startups and f\"\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"control\": 22,\n  \"ruler_question\": {\n   \"question\": \"Will Chinese authorities issue any authorization for Chinese companies to import Nvidia H200 chips by January 31, 2027?\",\n   \"unit\": \"yes/no\",\n   \"due_day\": \"2027-01-31\",\n   \"rungs\": [\n    {\n     \"label\": \"Yes\",\n     \"condition\": \"Chinese authorities publicly authorize at least one Chinese company to import Nvidia H200 chips by January 31, 2027.\",\n     \"lo\": null,\n     \"hi\": null\n    },\n    {\n     \"label\": \"No\",\n     \"condition\": \"Chinese authorities have not publicly authorized any Chinese company to import Nvidia H200 chips by January 31, 2027.\",\n     \"lo\": null,\n     \"hi\": null\n    }\n   ]\n  },\n  \"judged_question\": {\n   \"view_id\": \"v4\",\n   \"question\": \"Will China's government or a regulator permit NVIDIA to sell H200 AI chips to Chinese customers by 2027-01-31, as shown by an official Chinese decision or NVIDIA-confirmed H200 shipments or revenue to Chinese customers?\",\n   \"looks_at\": \"A Chinese government/regulator decision allowing H200 sales in China, evidenced by an official Chinese decision or by NVIDIA-confirmed H200 shipments/revenue to Chinese customers.\",\n   \"due_day\": \"2027-01-31\",\n   \"year\": \"2027\",\n   \"kind\": \"theme\"\n  },\n  \"judged_rungs\": [\n   {\n    \"position\": 1,\n    \"label\": \"Yes\",\n    \"condition\": \"By 2027-01-31, an official Chinese government or regulator decision permits H200 sales to Chinese customers, or NVIDIA confirms H200 shipments or revenue to Chinese customers.\",\n    \"lo\": null,\n    \"hi\": null\n   },\n   {\n    \"position\": 2,\n    \"label\": \"Ruled out\",\n    \"condition\": \"The Chinese government or a Chinese regulator formally denies permission for H200 sales to Chinese customers or formally prohibits those sales. This rung holds when that, or any other decision against the event of rung 1 as a whole, or a formal abandonment or withdrawal of it as a whole, was made after 2026-07-02 and stands on 2027-01-31, and only when rung 1 does not.\",\n    \"lo\": null,\n    \"hi\": null\n   },\n   {\n    \"position\": 3,\n    \"label\": \"Still open\",\n    \"condition\": \"Neither rung 1 nor rung 2 holds on 2027-01-31.\",\n    \"lo\": null,\n    \"hi\": null\n   }\n  ],\n  \"reader_b_decisive_items\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"i270\",\n    \"day\": \"2026-07-08\",\n    \"title\": \"China to let AI firms buy Nvidia H200s, says report\",\n    \"passage\": \"China plans to allow top artificial intelligence companies to buy a limited amount of H200 chips from Nvidia\",\n    \"meaning\": {\n     \"who\": \"Chinese officials\",\n     \"what\": \"permission for top AI companies (Alibaba, ByteDance, DeepSeek) to buy NVIDIA H200 chips, each on approval stating the number and reason\",\n     \"how_much\": \"a limited amount; total may be fewer than 200,000\",\n     \"condition\": \"approval per request; limited amount\",\n     \"status\": \"rumour\"\n    }\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"i304\",\n    \"day\": \"2026-07-14\",\n    \"title\": \"Nvidia slashes list of authorized customers in Asia in a bid to reduce AI chip smuggling, report claims — company sent field inspectors, called customers to check if business is genuine after pressure from Washington\",\n    \"passage\": \"Beijing refused to allow Chinese companies to buy these AI processors\",\n    \"meaning\": {\n     \"who\": \"Beijing\",\n     \"what\": \"Chinese companies not allowed to buy NVIDIA H200\",\n     \"status\": \"happened\"\n    }\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"i305\",\n    \"day\": \"2026-07-14\",\n    \"title\": \"Nvidia slashes list of authorized customers in Asia in a bid to reduce AI chip smuggling, report claims — company sent field inspectors, called customers to check if business is genuine after pressure from Washington\",\n    \"passage\": \"all domestic suppliers are sold out and that they’re even considering less powerful chips\",\n    \"meaning\": {\n     \"who\": \"unnamed Chinese tech executive\",\n     \"what\": \"China's domestic AI-chip suppliers are sold out; buyers are considering less powerful chips\",\n     \"status\": \"claim\"\n    }\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"i309\",\n    \"day\": \"2026-07-14\",\n    \"title\": \"Nvidia slashes list of authorized customers in Asia in a bid to reduce AI chip smuggling, report claims — company sent field inspectors, called customers to check if business is genuine after pressure from Washington\",\n    \"passage\": \"President Donald Trump took a 180-degree turn in December 2025 and finally allowed Nvidia to export its H200 GPUs to select customers in the region\",\n    \"meaning\": {\n     \"who\": \"US President Trump\",\n     \"what\": \"allowed H200 exports to select customers in China\",\n     \"period\": \"December 2025\",\n     \"status\": \"happened\"\n    }\n   }\n  ],\n  \"evidence_shown_to_the_judge_through_2026_07_14\": [\n   \"- e45 [fact; Noah Berman, The Wire (thewirechina.com); original source U.S. government (President Trump) ; from gnews:f8d63eab0337895e8e95cf02 2026-07-02] President Trump announced that his administration would allow NVIDIA to sell its H200 AI chips to China last December, after the U.S. government had restricted such sales under export controls the Biden administration put in place on the chip, which NVIDIA launched in 2023.\\n    Quote: President Trump announced that his administration would allow Nvidia to sell its H200 chips to China last December. The government had previously restricted Nvidia from selling China the chip, which it launched in 2023, due to export controls the Biden administration put in place.\",\n   \"- e46 [fact; Noah Berman, The Wire (thewirechina.com); original source Chinese government (as reported) ; from gnews:f8d63eab0337895e8e95cf02 2026-07-02] As of the 2026-07-02 article, the Chinese government had kept NVIDIA locked out of the China market despite the Trump administration having cleared the path for H200 exports.\\n    Quote: The Chinese government has to date locked Nvidia out of the China market, despite the Trump administration having cleared its path for H200 exports.\",\n   \"- e47 [party_statement; Noah Berman, The Wire (thewirechina.com); original source NVIDIA (CEO Jensen Huang) ; from gnews:f8d63eab0337895e8e95cf02 2026-07-02] NVIDIA CEO Jensen Huang has said NVIDIA's market share in China has fallen to zero since the Biden administration first imposed export controls on AI chips in 2022.\\n    Quote: Since the Biden administration first imposed export controls on AI chips in 2022, Nvidia’s market share in China has fallen to zero, Huang has said .\",\n   \"- e153 [party_statement; Omor Ibne Ehsan, 247wallst.com; original source NVIDIA (Colette Kress, CFO) ; from gnews:4d1b1ca014068fb532f116b3 2026-07-06] NVIDIA's China data center compute revenue is effectively zero, and CFO Colette Kress said losing that market — which NVIDIA sizes at \\\"close to about $50 billion in the future\\\" — would be material.\\n    Quote: China data center compute revenue is effectively zero, and Colette Kress (Nvidia’s CFO) said losing that market, which NVIDIA sizes at “close to about $50 billion in the future,” would be material.\",\n   \"- e183 [third_party_view; Bloomberg (Business Times); original source Bloomberg Intelligence (survey report) 2026-07-07; from gnews:8d5c91ae833af14257a59b2b 2026-07-06] A Bloomberg Intelligence survey of 60 executives at Chinese software, finance, manufacturing and retail companies, released on Tuesday Jul 7, found respondents plan to allocate 46% of their AI accelerator budget to domestic products over the next 12 months, up from 30% today; the report said China's drive to substitute locally made AI semiconductors for foreign ones is making progress and is likely to benefit domestic makers such as Huawei and Hygon.\\n    Quote: Executives in the country say they will allocate 46 per cent of their budget for AI accelerators to domestic products over the next 12 months. … This is up from 30 per cent today, according to a Bloomberg Intelligence survey released on Tuesday (Jul 7). … “China’s drive to substitute locally-made AI semiconductors for foreign ones is making progress, which is likely to benefit domestic makers such\",\n   \"- e184 [third_party_view; Bloomberg (Business Times); original source Bloomberg Intelligence (survey report) 2026-07-07; from gnews:8d5c91ae833af14257a59b2b 2026-07-06] The same Bloomberg Intelligence survey said Nvidia's products remain popular but its market share is expected to shrink as its H20 chips, which Beijing has urged Chinese tech firms not to use, become harder to find and local firms take over; Hygon Information Technology and Cambricon Technologies accelerators were being evaluated by a large pool of respondents, and Tencent, Alibaba and Huawei were named as best-positioned to gain from the shift.\\n    Quote: While Nvidia’s products remain popular, the Santa Clara-based firm’s market share is expected to shrink as its H20 chips, which Beijing has urged its tech firms not to use , become harder to find and local firms take over, according to the survey. … AI accelerators produced by Hygon Information Technology and Cambricon Technologies were also being evaluated by a large pool of respondents to the su\",\n   \"- e185 [fact; Bloomberg News; original source Chinese government ; from gnews:8d5c91ae833af14257a59b2b 2026-07-06] China is allocating roughly two trillion yuan (US$294 billion) to build data centres across the country over the next five years under a government-led initiative to expand AI use, and at least 80% of core technologies such as chips will be supplied by domestic companies, Bloomberg News reported.\\n    Quote: China is allocating roughly two trillion yuan (US$294 billion) to build data centres across the country in the next five years, a government-led initiative to expand the use of AI in sectors from healthcare to city management. … At least 80 per cent of core technologies, such as chips, will be supplied by domestic companies, Bloomberg News reported.\",\n   \"- e47 [party_statement; Noah Berman, The Wire (thewirechina.com); original source NVIDIA (CEO Jensen Huang) ; from gnews:f8d63eab0337895e8e95cf02 2026-07-02] NVIDIA CEO Jensen Huang has said NVIDIA's market share in China has fallen to zero since the Biden administration first imposed export controls on AI chips in 2022.\\n    Quote: Since the Biden administration first imposed export controls on AI chips in 2022, Nvidia’s market share in China has fallen to zero, Huang has said .\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e232; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e183 [third_party_view; Bloomberg (Business Times); original source Bloomberg Intelligence (survey report) 2026-07-07; from gnews:8d5c91ae833af14257a59b2b 2026-07-06] A Bloomberg Intelligence survey of 60 executives at Chinese software, finance, manufacturing and retail companies, released on Tuesday Jul 7, found respondents plan to allocate 46% of their AI accelerator budget to domestic products over the next 12 months, up from 30% today; the report said China's drive to substitute locally made AI semiconductors for foreign ones is making progress and is likely to benefit domestic makers such as Huawei and Hygon.\\n    Quote: Executives in the country say they will allocate 46 per cent of their budget for AI accelerators to domestic products over the next 12 months. … This is up from 30 per cent today, according to a Bloomberg Intelligence survey released on Tuesday (Jul 7). … “China’s drive to substitute locally-made AI semiconductors for foreign ones is making progress, which is likely to benefit domestic makers such\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e227; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e232 [third_party_view; idnfinancials.com; original source Richard Windsor, Radio Free Mobile ; from gnews:2957e5c79a365952140c8cce 2026-07-07] Analyst Richard Windsor of Radio Free Mobile said Nvidia's position in China is \\\"effectively at zero and will remain that way\\\" and that DeepSeek has almost no chance of selling chips outside China unless it gains access to advanced manufacturing technology, adding that DeepSeek's chip development would not affect Nvidia.\\n    Quote: \\\"Nvidia's position in China is effectively at zero and will remain that way. DeepSeek has almost no chance of selling chips outside China unless it gains access to advanced manufacturing technology,\\\" said analyst Richard Windsor of Radio Free Mobile, adding that the development would not affect the chipmaker.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e47; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e46 [fact; Noah Berman, The Wire (thewirechina.com); original source Chinese government (as reported) ; from gnews:f8d63eab0337895e8e95cf02 2026-07-02] As of the 2026-07-02 article, the Chinese government had kept NVIDIA locked out of the China market despite the Trump administration having cleared the path for H200 exports.\\n    Quote: The Chinese government has to date locked Nvidia out of the China market, despite the Trump administration having cleared its path for H200 exports.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e238; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e183 [third_party_view; Bloomberg (Business Times); original source Bloomberg Intelligence (survey report) 2026-07-07; from gnews:8d5c91ae833af14257a59b2b 2026-07-06] A Bloomberg Intelligence survey of 60 executives at Chinese software, finance, manufacturing and retail companies, released on Tuesday Jul 7, found respondents plan to allocate 46% of their AI accelerator budget to domestic products over the next 12 months, up from 30% today; the report said China's drive to substitute locally made AI semiconductors for foreign ones is making progress and is likely to benefit domestic makers such as Huawei and Hygon.\\n    Quote: Executives in the country say they will allocate 46 per cent of their budget for AI accelerators to domestic products over the next 12 months. … This is up from 30 per cent today, according to a Bloomberg Intelligence survey released on Tuesday (Jul 7). … “China’s drive to substitute locally-made AI semiconductors for foreign ones is making progress, which is likely to benefit domestic makers such\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e227, e267; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e184 [third_party_view; Bloomberg (Business Times); original source Bloomberg Intelligence (survey report) 2026-07-07; from gnews:8d5c91ae833af14257a59b2b 2026-07-06] The same Bloomberg Intelligence survey said Nvidia's products remain popular but its market share is expected to shrink as its H20 chips, which Beijing has urged Chinese tech firms not to use, become harder to find and local firms take over; Hygon Information Technology and Cambricon Technologies accelerators were being evaluated by a large pool of respondents, and Tencent, Alibaba and Huawei were named as best-positioned to gain from the shift.\\n    Quote: While Nvidia’s products remain popular, the Santa Clara-based firm’s market share is expected to shrink as its H20 chips, which Beijing has urged its tech firms not to use , become harder to find and local firms take over, according to the survey. … AI accelerators produced by Hygon Information Technology and Cambricon Technologies were also being evaluated by a large pool of respondents to the su\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e270; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e185 [fact; Bloomberg News; original source Chinese government ; from gnews:8d5c91ae833af14257a59b2b 2026-07-06] China is allocating roughly two trillion yuan (US$294 billion) to build data centres across the country over the next five years under a government-led initiative to expand AI use, and at least 80% of core technologies such as chips will be supplied by domestic companies, Bloomberg News reported.\\n    Quote: China is allocating roughly two trillion yuan (US$294 billion) to build data centres across the country in the next five years, a government-led initiative to expand the use of AI in sectors from healthcare to city management. … At least 80 per cent of core technologies, such as chips, will be supplied by domestic companies, Bloomberg News reported.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e271; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e236 [rumour; The Information, as reported by The Straits Times; original source The Information 2026-07-08; from gnews:c8c39f971721fd820aab5c1c 2026-07-08] The Information reported on 8 July 2026, citing two unidentified people familiar with the matter, that Chinese officials have told Alibaba Group, ByteDance and DeepSeek they will have permission to buy limited quantities of Nvidia's H200 AI chips, easing prior restrictions. Buyers must request approval specifying how many chips they need and why, and the Chinese government is still deciding the total allowed, which may end up fewer than 200,000 chips.\\n    Quote: China plans to allow top artificial intelligence companies to buy a limited amount of H200 chips from Nvidia, a sign the country is easing restrictions on the coveted US technology, according to the Information news site. … Chinese officials have informed companies such as Alibaba Group, ByteDance and DeepSeek that they will have permission to purchase some of the processors, which are used to dev\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read it as a later version of e46 (); both are in force: As of the 2026-07-02 article, the Chinese government had kept NVIDIA locked out of the China market despite the Trump administration having cleared the path for H200 exports.\",\n   \"- e238 [fact; The Straits Times; original source U.S. President Donald Trump ; from gnews:c8c39f971721fd820aab5c1c 2026-07-08] US President Donald Trump granted Nvidia permission in December to sell the processors (H200 AI chips) to China, but Chinese authorities have been slow to allow the technology into the country, the article reports.\\n    Quote: Though US President Donald Trump granted Nvidia permission to sell the processors to China in December, the Chinese authorities have been slow to allow the technology into the country.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e46; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e239 [party_statement; The Straits Times; original source Colette Kress, Nvidia CFO ; from gnews:c8c39f971721fd820aab5c1c 2026-07-08] In May, Nvidia chief financial officer Colette Kress said the H200 chips had yet to generate any revenue for the company in China, and that Nvidia was uncertain whether any imports would be allowed into the country.\\n    Quote: In May, Nvidia chief financial officer Colette Kress said the H200 chips had yet to generate any revenue for the company in China. … “We are uncertain whether any imports will be allowed into the country,” she said.\",\n   \"- e236 [rumour; The Information, as reported by The Straits 