on from China Data Center compute revenue due to ongoing U.S. export restrictions.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e131; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e315 [rumour; MIT Technology Review (The Download); original source The Information ; from gnews:50a84fa6b2f3eb33f29948a4 2026-07-09] The Information reported that China plans to let its top AI firms buy Nvidia's H200 AI chips, with Alibaba, ByteDance and DeepSeek set to receive permission; Reuters reported that China had previously withheld approval even though the U.S. had authorized the sales.\\n    Quote: China plans to let its top AI firms buy Nvidia H200 chips\",\n   \"- e316 [fact; South China Morning Post; original source U.S. government (Washington) ; from gnews:91684b863d274b10246d0c13 2026-07-09] In early 2026, the U.S. government (Washington) took the step of approving Nvidia's H200 graphics processing unit for export to China, according to the South China Morning Post.\\n    Quote: In early 2026, Washington took the rare step of approving Nvidia’s H200 graphics processing unit for export to China\",\n   \"- e317 [rumour; South China Morning Post; original source Unnamed source with knowledge of the matter ; from gnews:91684b863d274b10246d0c13 2026-07-09] An unnamed source with knowledge of the matter told the South China Morning Post that China's government plans to let selected companies, including Alibaba Group Holding, buy limited numbers of Nvidia's H200 GPUs, as Beijing's stance begins to change.\\n    Quote: Now, however, China’s stance is beginning to change, as the government plans to let selected companies – including Alibaba Group Holding – buy limited numbers of the device, a source with knowledge of the matter told the South China Morning Post.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e236; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e340 [fact; moomoo; original source China government ; from gnews:7b39859113d4e2b8ebd8aa86 2026-07-09] China is moving to grant limited approval for domestic firms to purchase Nvidia H200 processors, easing access to Nvidia's technology, though companies must now request individual approval for specific quantities and use cases.\\n    Quote: The stock is also navigating a complex geopolitical environment as China moves to grant limited approval for domestic firms to purchase H200 processors. While this policy shift eases access to Nvidia’s technology, companies must now request individual approval for specific quantities and use cases.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e236; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e356 [party_statement; Vandita Jadeja (247wallst.com); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:f0423de52e9bd77b530ba76e 2026-07-10] NVIDIA's Q2 FY27 guidance explicitly excludes Data Center compute revenue from China, and the company reported zero H20 shipments in the quarter.\\n    Quote: Q2 FY27 guidance explicitly excludes Data Center compute revenue from China, and there were zero H20 shipments in the quarter.\",\n   \"- e362 [party_statement; David Moadel, 24/7 Wall St.; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:cbb9ff1a37ebb451f9321da5 2026-07-10] NVIDIA management guided Q2 FY2027 revenue to $91 billion, an outlook the company said assumed zero Data Center compute revenue from China; the article notes that any policy thaw on H200 exports could therefore be additional upside.\\n    Quote: Non-GAAP gross margin came in at 75%, and management guided Q2 FY2027 revenue to $91 billion. … Notably, that outlook assumed zero Data Center compute revenue from China, meaning any policy thaw on H200 exports could represent additional upside.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e131; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e183 [third_party_view; Bloomberg (Business Times); original source Bloomberg Intelligence (survey report) 2026-07-07; from gnews:8d5c91ae833af14257a59b2b 2026-07-06] A Bloomberg Intelligence survey of 60 executives at Chinese software, finance, manufacturing and retail companies, released on Tuesday Jul 7, found respondents plan to allocate 46% of their AI accelerator budget to domestic products over the next 12 months, up from 30% today; the report said China's drive to substitute locally made AI semiconductors for foreign ones is making progress and is likely to benefit domestic makers such as Huawei and Hygon.\\n    Quote: Executives in the country say they will allocate 46 per cent of their budget for AI accelerators to domestic products over the next 12 months. … This is up from 30 per cent today, according to a Bloomberg Intelligence survey released on Tuesday (Jul 7). … “China’s drive to substitute locally-made AI semiconductors for foreign ones is making progress, which is likely to benefit domestic makers such\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e227, e267, e425; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e356 [party_statement; Vandita Jadeja (247wallst.com); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:f0423de52e9bd77b530ba76e 2026-07-10] NVIDIA's Q2 FY27 guidance explicitly excludes Data Center compute revenue from China, and the company reported zero H20 shipments in the quarter.\\n    Quote: Q2 FY27 guidance explicitly excludes Data Center compute revenue from China, and there were zero H20 shipments in the quarter.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e427; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e427 [party_statement; barchart.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:ededac685b09f7a9c6952202 2026-07-12] Nvidia management guided second-quarter revenue to approximately $91 billion, despite assuming zero China data-center revenue because of export restrictions, signaling that the export restrictions remain binding on its China data-center business at the time of guidance.\\n    Quote: Looking ahead, management guided Q2 revenue to approximately $91 billion, despite assuming zero China data-center revenue because of export restrictions.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e356; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e356 [party_statement; Vandita Jadeja (247wallst.com); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:f0423de52e9bd77b530ba76e 2026-07-10] NVIDIA's Q2 FY27 guidance explicitly excludes Data Center compute revenue from China, and the company reported zero H20 shipments in the quarter.\\n    Quote: Q2 FY27 guidance explicitly excludes Data Center compute revenue from China, and there were zero H20 shipments in the quarter.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e427, e447; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e447 [party_statement; Alex Sirois, 247wallst.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:799ec454c15ddf61f08d042f 2026-07-13] The article states that NVIDIA's guidance for its Q2 assumes zero China Data Center compute revenue, a headwind it says Broadcom does not carry to the same degree.\\n    Quote: NVIDIA leans on scale and CUDA gravity, but assumes zero China Data Center compute revenue in Q2 guidance, a headwind Broadcom does not carry to the same degree.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e356; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e458 [fact; Vandita Jadeja (247wallst.com); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:b68c019528300c6a1fcd3515 2026-07-13] NVIDIA stated that no H20 compute products were shipped to China in Q1 FY27 and that its Q2 FY27 guidance excludes any Data Center compute revenue from China, indicating China data-center sales remain blocked.\\n    Quote: There were no H20 compute products shipped to China in Q1 FY27, and the Q2 FY27 guidance excludes any Data Center compute revenue from China.\",\n   \"- e464 [fact; 24/7 Wall St. (Alex Sirois); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:221f52333c8f5e936e515c4d 2026-07-13] NVIDIA shipped zero H20 AI chips to China in the latest quarter, versus $4.6 billion of H20 revenue from China in the year-ago period, the article reports.\\n    Quote: Yet zero H20 chips shipped to China this quarter, versus $4.6 billion a year ago.\",\n   \"- e45 [fact; Noah Berman, The Wire (thewirechina.com); original source U.S. government (President Trump) ; from gnews:f8d63eab0337895e8e95cf02 2026-07-02] President Trump announced that his administration would allow NVIDIA to sell its H200 AI chips to China last December, after the U.S. government had restricted such sales under export controls the Biden administration put in place on the chip, which NVIDIA launched in 2023.\\n    Quote: President Trump announced that his administration would allow Nvidia to sell its H200 chips to China last December. The government had previously restricted Nvidia from selling China the chip, which it launched in 2023, due to export controls the Biden administration put in place.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e500; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e316 [fact; South China Morning Post; original source U.S. government (Washington) ; from gnews:91684b863d274b10246d0c13 2026-07-09] In early 2026, the U.S. government (Washington) took the step of approving Nvidia's H200 graphics processing unit for export to China, according to the South China Morning Post.\\n    Quote: In early 2026, Washington took the rare step of approving Nvidia’s H200 graphics processing unit for export to China\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e471; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e356 [party_statement; Vandita Jadeja (247wallst.com); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:f0423de52e9bd77b530ba76e 2026-07-10] NVIDIA's Q2 FY27 guidance explicitly excludes Data Center compute revenue from China, and the company reported zero H20 shipments in the quarter.\\n    Quote: Q2 FY27 guidance explicitly excludes Data Center compute revenue from China, and there were zero H20 shipments in the quarter.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e427, e447, e473, e494, e508; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e470 [party_statement; CNBC; original source Jeffery Kessler, Under Secretary of Commerce for Industry and Security (U.S. Department of Commerce) 2026-07-14; from gnews:9d898f2bf43ed52a98e1587a 2026-07-14] At a congressional hearing on Tuesday (2026-07-14), Under Secretary of Commerce for Industry and Security Jeffery Kessler said \\\"very few\\\" of Nvidia's H200 AI chips have been shipped to China and Hong Kong, describing it as \\\"a very small quantity of chips\\\" and that \\\"very few shipments against licenses for H200s and equivalents have taken place.\\\" The article reports this as a sign H200 shipments to China have restarted. Nvidia declined to comment.\\n    Quote: A top U.S. trade official said on Tuesday that \\\"very few\\\" of Nvidia's H200 AI chips have been shipped to China and Hong Kong. … \\\"The bottom line is very few shipments against licenses for H200s and equivalents have taken place. It's a very small quantity of chips,\\\" Under Secretary of Commerce for Industry and Security Jeffery Kessler said at a congressional hearing. … The remark is a sign that H20\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e484, e530; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read it as a later version of e46 (); both are in force: As of the 2026-07-02 article, the Chinese government had kept NVIDIA locked out of the China market despite the Trump administration having cleared the path for H200 exports.\",\n   \"- e471 [fact; CNBC; original source President Donald Trump / U.S. government ; from gnews:9d898f2bf43ed52a98e1587a 2026-07-14] In December, President Donald Trump said the U.S. government would approve China sales of the H200 AI chip in exchange for a 25% cut, and licenses for the chips were issued earlier this year (2026); some in the administration say the chips can be used for military purposes. This describes the U.S. approval policy for H200 China sales currently in force.\\n    Quote: In December, President Donald Trump said that the U.S. government would approve China sales of the H200 AI chip in exchange for a 25% cut. Licenses for the chips, which some in the administration say can be used for military purposes, were issued earlier this year .\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e316; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e472 [party_statement; CNBC; original source Jeffery Kessler, Under Secretary of Commerce for Industry and Security (U.S. Department of Commerce) 2026-07-14; from gnews:9d898f2bf43ed52a98e1587a 2026-07-14] Kessler said the U.S. government assesses companies that want H200 chips on a case-by-case basis, with applicants needing to meet national security requirements and submit to inspections to ensure the chips are compliant, and that \\\"there are cases where we deny the license applications we receive.\\\"\\n    Quote: Kessler said that the U.S. government assessed companies that want the H200 chips on a case-by-case basis, with applicants needing to meet national security requirements and submit to inspections to make sure the chips are compliant. … \\\"There are cases where we deny the license applications we receive,\\\" Kessler said.\",\n   \"- e473 [party_statement; CNBC; original source Nvidia / CEO Jensen Huang ; from gnews:9d898f2bf43ed52a98e1587a 2026-07-14] Since last year Nvidia has excluded any potential Chinese AI chip revenue from its forecasts, and CEO Jensen Huang said on CNBC in May that he told investors to \\\"expect nothing\\\" from Chinese sales. Nvidia's guidance therefore