Vandita Jadeja (247wallst.com); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:1e1fa5a194198eaf05d90c9e 2026-07-14] NVIDIA's guidance excludes any Data Center compute revenue from China, which the article calls a real overhang.\\n    Quote: Guidance also excludes any Data Center compute revenue from China, a real overhang.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e356; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e500 [fact; Jowi Morales, Tom's Hardware, citing the Financial Times; original source U.S. government (President Donald Trump) ; from gnews:2a41a34c16f5280b624481fb 2026-07-14] President Donald Trump reversed course in December 2025 and allowed Nvidia to export its H200 GPUs to select customers in the region, per the article; it says this would have alleviated the Chinese supply situation had Beijing not blocked Chinese companies from buying the processors.\\n    Quote: President Donald Trump took a 180-degree turn in December 2025 and finally allowed Nvidia to export its H200 GPUs to select customers in the region, which would have alleviated the situation.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e45; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read it as a later version of e33 (); both are in force: The US has since approved the sale of some of Nvidia's semiconductors to China under certain conditions, the article reports, following Washington's 2022 restriction of their export over concerns the chips could be used by the Chinese military — a regulatory posture in force bearing on Nvidia's China market access.\",\n   \"- e508 [party_statement; Vandita Jadeja, 247wallst.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:184645c8d683d95b684bd6da 2026-07-14] NVIDIA guided Q2 revenue to $91 billion, a forecast management said assumes zero China Data Center compute revenue.\\n    Quote: NVIDIA guided Q2 to $91 billion in revenue, which assumes zero China Data Center compute.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e356; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e517 [rumour; Reuters; original source U.S. government (Commerce Department/Bureau of Industry and Security) ; from gnews:24268bfaab6ef1d7876b188c 2026-07-14] U.S. authorities have granted approval to ZTE Kangxun Telecom and server maker Maginfra to purchase Nvidia's H200 AI chips, and cleared Zhuhai Hengqin Yunxiang Zhisheng Network Technology, a Kingsoft subsidiary, to use some AMD chips that rival the H200, according to documents and two unnamed sources. The three firms had not previously been reported to have received U.S. clearance, expanding the known set of licensed buyers beyond China's largest internet groups and major electronics distributors. ZTE, Maginfra, Kingsoft, Nvidia, AMD and China's Ministry of Commerce did not respond to requests for comment; BIS did not immediately reply.\\n    Quote: A unit of telecoms gear maker ZTE Corp and two other Chinese firms are among the latest entities to receive U.S. approval to purchase ‌advanced AI chips from Nvidia and AMD, according to documents and two sources ‌familiar with the matter. … ZTE Kangxun Telecom and server maker Maginfra have been permitted to purchase Nvidia's H200 chips, while Zhuhai Hengqin Yunxiang Zhisheng Network Technology, \\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e525, e526; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e518 [rumour; Reuters; original source Chinese cloud computing firms (unnamed) / Chinese authorities on import review ; from gnews:24268bfaab6ef1d7876b188c 2026-07-14] Some Chinese cloud firms have recently told partners and clients they may soon be able to obtain Nvidia's H200 chips, according to the two unnamed sources, which Reuters said indicates some progress in the import reviews by Chinese authorities.\\n    Quote: However, some Chinese cloud firms have recently told partners and clients they may soon be able to obtain H200 chips, the sources said, indicating some progress in import reviews by Chinese authorities.\",\n   \"- e525 [fact; Reuters (relayed by Tom's Hardware / Bruno Ferreira); original source U.S. government ; from gnews:3221eba2826b001b12b82770 2026-07-14] Reuters reported that Chinese telecoms giant ZTE and server firm Maginfra received U.S. government approval to buy Nvidia's last-generation H200 'Hopper' AI chips, joining Alibaba, Tencent, ByteDance and JD.com among roughly 10 Chinese companies with U.S. clearance for such purchases.\\n    Quote: Today, Reuters reports that Chinese telecoms giant ZTE and server firm Maginfra have received U.S. approval to buy Nvidia's last-gen H200 \\\"Hopper\\\" chips. … ZTE joins a club that counts Alibaba, Tencent, ByteDance, and JD.com among the roughly 10-strong group of Chinese companies with U.S. clearance for those purchases.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e517; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read it as a later version of e518 (); both are in force: Some Chinese cloud firms have recently told partners and clients they may soon be able to obtain Nvidia's H200 chips, according to the two unnamed sources, which Reuters said indicates some progress in the import reviews by Chinese authorities.\",\n   \"- e527 [third_party_view; Reuters (relayed by Tom's Hardware / Bruno Ferreira); original source Chinese government (stance/approval status), per Reuters ; from gnews:3221eba2826b001b12b82770 2026-07-14] Reuters reported that there was no word on whether Chinese authorities would give ZTE the go-ahead to import the H200 chips, noting China has taken a protectionist stance, discouraging firms from buying foreign tech and pushing them toward homegrown accelerators such as Huawei's.\\n    Quote: Over on the Chinese side of the table, Reuters remarks that there's no word on whether the respective authorities will give ZTE the go-ahead for import, as the country has taken on a protectionist stance as it tries to grow its own chip industry. The country has discouraged firms from purchasing foreign tech and has instead pushed companies to acquire homegrown accelerators.\",\n   \"- e528 [third_party_view; Reuters (relayed by Tom's Hardware / Bruno Ferreira); original source Reuters (reported Chinese order book) ; from gnews:3221eba2826b001b12b82770 2026-07-14] Reuters reported, around six months before the 2026-07-14 article, that Chinese tech firms had more than two million H200 chips on order, far more than Nvidia had on hand at the time.\\n    Quote: But even with those domestic production initiatives, the Chinese hunger for AI silicon is so deep that six months ago, Reuters said the nation's tech firms had more than two million H200 chips on order, far more than what Nvidia had on hand at the time.\",\n   \"- e529 [fact; Tom's Hardware (Bruno Ferreira); original source U.S. government ; from gnews:3221eba2826b001b12b82770 2026-07-14] The item describes the standing U.S. export-control framework: U.S. authorities allow Chinese firms to buy AI chips up to and including the Hopper family (meaning no Blackwell chips), with a 25% export tariff, with final decisions made case-by-case.\\n    Quote: The current status of the AI chip trade situation is roughly that the U.S. allows Chinese firms to buy AI chips up to and including the Hopper family (meaning no Blackwell chips), with a 25% export tariff, though final decisions are made on a case-by-case basis.\",\n   \"- e530 [party_statement; CNBC (relayed by Tom's Hardware); original source U.S. trade official ; from gnews:3221eba2826b001b12b82770 2026-07-14] A U.S. trade official, cited by CNBC at a congressional hearing, stated that 'very few shipments against licenses for H200s and equivalents have taken place. It's a very small quantity of chips.'\\n    Quote: CNBC cites a U.S. trade official who today stated that \\\"very few shipments against licenses for H200s and equivalents have taken place. It’s a very small quantity of chips\\\" during a congressional hearing.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e470; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\"\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"control\": 23,\n  \"ruler_question\": {\n   \"question\": \"How much will the four largest AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026?\",\n   \"unit\": \"USD\",\n   \"due_day\": \"2027-12-31\",\n   \"rungs\": [\n    {\n     \"label\": \"Below $550 billion\",\n     \"condition\": \"Combined spending is less than $550 billion.\",\n     \"lo\": null,\n     \"hi\": 550000000000.0\n    },\n    {\n     \"label\": \"$550 billion to below $600 billion\",\n     \"condition\": \"Combined spending is at least $550 billion and less than $600 billion.\",\n     \"lo\": 550000000000.0,\n     \"hi\": 600000000000.0\n    },\n    {\n     \"label\": \"$600 billion to below $700 billion\",\n     \"condition\": \"Combined spending is at least $600 billion and less than $700 billion.\",\n     \"lo\": 600000000000.0,\n     \"hi\": 700000000000.0\n    },\n    {\n     \"label\": \"$700 billion to below $800 billion\",\n     \"condition\": \"Combined spending is at least $700 billion and less than $800 billion.\",\n     \"lo\": 700000000000.0,\n     \"hi\": 800000000000.0\n    },\n    {\n     \"label\": \"$800 billion or more\",\n     \"condition\": \"Combined spending is at least $800 billion.\",\n     \"lo\": 800000000000.0,\n     \"hi\": null\n    }\n   ]\n  },\n  \"judged_question\": {\n   \"view_id\": \"v6\",\n   \"question\": \"How much did the big four AI hyperscalers spend in combined data center capital expenditures during calendar 2026, according to their full-year 2026 capex disclosures?\",\n   \"looks_at\": \"The sum of calendar-2026 data center capex actually reported by the big four AI hyperscalers, versus the roughly $650 billion they are reported to plan for this year.\",\n   \"due_day\": \"2027-03-31\",\n   \"year\": \"2026\",\n   \"kind\": \"theme\"\n  },\n  \"judged_rungs\": [\n   {\n    \"position\": 1,\n    \"label\": \"below 500,000,000,000 USD\",\n    \"condition\": \"below 500,000,000,000 USD\",\n    \"lo\": null,\n    \"hi\": 500000000000.0\n   },\n   {\n    \"position\": 2,\n    \"label\": \"from 500,000,000,000 USD to below 600,000,000,000 USD\",\n    \"condition\": \"from 500,000,000,000 USD to below 600,000,000,000 USD\",\n    \"lo\": 500000000000.0,\n    \"hi\": 600000000000.0\n   },\n   {\n    \"position\": 3,\n    \"label\": \"from 600,000,000,000 USD to below 700,000,000,000 USD\",\n    \"condition\": \"from 600,000,000,000 USD to below 700,000,000,000 USD (the reference when the question opened: \\\"plan to spend around $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year\\\")\",\n    \"lo\": 600000000000.0,\n    \"hi\": 700000000000.0\n   },\n   {\n    \"position\": 4,\n    \"label\": \"from 700,000,000,000 USD to below 800,000,000,000 USD\",\n    \"condition\": \"from 700,000,000,000 USD to below 800,000,000,000 USD\",\n    \"lo\": 700000000000.0,\n    \"hi\": 800000000000.0\n   },\n   {\n    \"position\": 5,\n    \"label\": \"800,000,000,000 USD or more\",\n    \"condition\": \"800,000,000,000 USD or more\",\n    \"lo\": 800000000000.0,\n    \"hi\": null\n   }\n  ],\n  \"reader_b_decisive_items\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"i256\",\n    \"day\": \"2026-07-07\",\n    \"title\": \"Nvidia Stock Is Down 17% From Its High. Is the Artificial Intelligence (AI) Leader Finally Cheap?\",\n    \"passage\": \"This year, the big four AI hyperscalers plan to spend around $650 billion on data center capital expenditures . Next year, Nvidia claims this group will spend more than $1 trillion.\",\n    \"meaning\": {\n     \"who\": \"big four AI hyperscalers' own plans (2026); NVIDIA's claim (2027)\",\n     \"what\": \"data-center capital expenditure of the big four\",\n     \"how_much\": \"around $650bn (2026); more than $1 trillion (2027)\",\n     \"period\": \"calendar 2026; calendar 2027\",\n     \"status\": \"plan (2026); forecast by NVIDIA (2027)\"\n    }\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"i232\",\n    \"day\": \"2026-07-07\",\n    \"title\": \"A Circuit Board Problem Just Delayed Nvidia's Next AI System to 2028 And Chip Stocks Are Already Feeling the Fallout\",\n    \"passage\": \"2026 capex is guided up tp $190 billion\",\n    \"meaning\": {\n     \"who\": \"Alphabet / Google\",\n     \"what\": \"capital expenditure guidance\",\n     \"how_much\": \"up to $190bn\",\n     \"period\": \"2026\",\n     \"status\": \"forecast (guidance)\"\n    }\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"i233\",\n    \"day\": \"2026-07-07\",\n    \"title\": \"A Circuit Board Problem Just Delayed Nvidia's Next AI System to 2028 And Chip Stocks Are Already Feeling the Fallout\",\n    \"passage\": \"Google Cloud revenue rose 63% with backlog nearly doubling to over $460 billion\",\n    \"meaning\": {\n     \"who\": \"Google Cloud\",\n     \"what\": \"revenue growth and backlog\",\n     \"how_much\": \"revenue +63%; backlog nearly doubled to over $460bn\",\n     \"period\": \"latest reported quarter\",\n     \"status\": \"happened\"\n    }\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"i295\",\n    \"day\": \"2026-07-10\",\n    \"title\": \"Here are Friday's biggest analyst calls: Nvidia, SpaceX, Toll, Shopify, Twilio, Seagate, Chipotle & more\",\n    \"passage\": \"the company describing \\\"accelerating growth rates\\\" even as revenues approach the $100 bn per quarter mark\",\n    \"meaning\": {\n     \"who\": \"NVIDIA management, relayed by Morgan Stanley\",\n     \"what\": \"revenue growth rates accelerating\",\n     \"how_much\": \"quarterly revenue approaching $100 bn\",\n     \"status\": \"claim\"\n    }\n   }\n  ],\n  \"evidence_shown_to_the_judge_through_2026_07_14\": [\n   \"- e82 [party_statement; fool.com (Motley Fool) article author; original source The big four AI hyperscalers ; from gnews:92c7e54d38dc2916aa185568 2026-07-04] The article reports that the big four AI hyperscalers plan to spend around $650 billion on data center capital expenditures in the current year (2026). This is a planned spending level by the customer group, not a committed order for NVIDIA.\\n    Quote: The big four AI hyperscalers plan to spend around $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year.\",\n   \"- e83 [party_statement; fool.com (Motley Fool) article author; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:92c7e54d38dc2916aa185568 2026-07-04] NVIDIA projects that the big four AI hyperscalers' combined data center capital expenditures could reach $1 trillion next year (2027), up from the roughly $650 billion planned for 2026. This is NVIDIA's own projection, not a reported outcome.\\n    Quote: The big four AI hyperscalers plan to spend around $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year. Next year, Nvidia projects that this figure could reach $1 trillion.\",\n   \"- e55 [fact; Tom's Hardware; original source  ; from gnews:b680fbe2c3e9228eba33a0b4 2026-07-02] Tom's Hardware reported that public opposition to data centers — blamed for large increases in power/utility costs and water consumption, with 7 in 10 Americans saying they do not want a data center in their backyard — led to the delay or cancellation of at least 75 data center projects in the first quarter of 2026, a demand signal for AI infrastructure.\\n    Quote: This has become a major national issue recently, with data centers being blamed for massive increases in power and utility costs, as well as increased water consumption and a reduction in the quality of life in the communities that surround these developments. … These problems have caused Americans to push back against these projects, with 7 out of 10 saying that they do not want a data center in \",\n   \"- e83 [party_statement; fool.com (Motley Fool) article author; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:92c7e54d38dc2916aa185568 2026-07-04] NVIDIA projects that the big four AI hyperscalers' combined data center capital expenditures could reach $1 trillion next year (2027), up from the roughly $650 billion planned for 2026. This is NVIDIA's own projection, not a reported outcome.\\n    Quote: The big four AI hyperscalers plan to spend around $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year. Next year, Nvidia projects that this figure could reach $1 trillion.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e125; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e55 [fact; Tom's Hardware; original source  ; from gnews:b680fbe2c3e9228eba33a0b4 2026-07-02] Tom's Hardware reported that public opposition to data centers — blamed for large increases in power/utility costs and water consumption, with 7 in 10 Americans saying they do not want a data center in their backyard — led to the delay or cancellation of at least 75 data center projects in the first quarter of 2026, a demand signal for AI infrastructure.\\n    Quote: This has become a major national issue recently, with data centers being blamed for massive increases in power and utility costs, as well as increased water consumption and a reduction in the quality of life in the communities that surround these developments. … These problems have caused Americans to push back against these projects, with 7 out of 10 saying that they do not want a data center in \\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e161; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e125 [party_statement; Keithen Drury (fool.com); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:71df54965281684cc500960f 2026-07-06] On its most recent quarterly conference call, NVIDIA management forecast that hyperscaler spending would top $1 trillion in 2027, after reaching a projected $650 billion in 2026.\\n    Quote: During its last quarterly conference call, management forecast that it expects hyperscaler spending to top $1 trillion in 2027 after reaching a projected $650 billion in 2026 .\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e83; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e158 [third_party_view; Allen Harris, The Berkshire Edge; original source Goldman Sachs ; from gnews:54aa9ce326115cedf8857512 2026-07-06] Goldman Sachs estimates annual AI infrastructure capital expenditures could reach $765 billion in 2026 and grow to $1.6 trillion by 2031, for cumulative spending of about $7.6 trillion from 2026 through 2031. The spending spans chips, data centers, power, cooling and networking required to run AI at scale.\\n    Quote: Goldman Sachs estimates annual AI infrastructure capital expenditures could reach $765 billion in 2026 and grow to $1.6 trillion by 2031, with cumulative spending of about $7.6 trillion from 2026 through 2031. That spending includes chips, data centers, power, cooling, networking, and the other plumbing required to run AI at scale.\",\n   \"- e161 [third_party_view; autonocion.com, citing Tom's Hardware; original source Tom's Hardware ; from gnews:417f6244f0aaa27123023deb 2026-07-06] More than 75 data center projects worth around $130 billion have been blocked or delayed across the US so far this year, according to a running tally at Tom's Hardware, and polling cited in the same reporting found 7 in 10 Americans oppose a data center built near their home.\\n    Quote: With over $130 billion in data center projects already blocked or delayed by communities fed up with power and water demands, a zero-local-water nuclear option isn't just an engineering story — it's a potential unlock for the entire AI buildout. … More than 75 of these projects, worth around $130 billion, have been blocked or delayed across the US so far this year, according to a running tally at \\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e55; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e170 [party_statement; Mizuho analyst Jordan Klein, reported by Investing.com (uk.finance.yahoo.com); original source Hon Hai Precision Industry Co Ltd (management) ; from gnews:d5c48abb470f394f86549b6b 2026-07-06] Hon Hai Precision Industry, one of Nvidia's largest suppliers, reported strong earnings on the Friday before the 2026-07-06 article and its management forecast a continued acceleration in AI demand; Mizuho analyst Jordan Klein called this \\\"a very good sign into the NVDA launch of their new Vera Rubin rack.\\\" No figures for the results were given.\\n    Quote: The analyst additionally pointed to strong earnings last Friday from Hon Hai Precision Industry Co Ltd (TW:2317), one of Nvidia's largest suppliers. Klein noted that Hon Hai management forecasted a continued acceleration in AI demand, which the analyst said was \\\"a very good sign into the NVDA launch of their new Vera Rubin rack.\\\"\",\n   \"- e82 [party_statement; fool.com (Motley Fool) article author; original source The big four AI hyperscalers ; from gnews:92c7e54d38dc2916aa185568 2026-07-04] The article reports that the big four AI hyperscalers plan to spend around $650 billion on data center capital expenditures in the current year (2026). This is a planned spending level by the customer group, not a committed order for NVIDIA.\\n    Quote: The big four AI hyperscalers plan to spend around $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e196; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e83 [party_statement; fool.com (Motley Fool) article author; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:92c7e54d38dc2916aa185568 2026-07-04] NVIDIA projects that the big four AI hyperscalers' combined data center capital expenditures could reach $1 trillion next year (2027), up from the roughly $650 billion planned for 2026. This is NVIDIA's own projection, not a reported outcome.\\n    Quote: The big four AI hyperscalers plan to spend around $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year. Next year, Nvidia projects that this figure could reach $1 trillion.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e125, e197, e205; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e196 [third_party_view; fool.com; original source fool.com author (aggregating the big four AI hyperscalers' capex plans) ; from gnews:f8191f5bb960d79e47c73d6c 2026-07-07] The article states that in calendar 2026 the big four AI hyperscalers plan to spend around $650 billion on data center capital expenditures.\\n    Quote: This year, the big four AI hyperscalers plan to spend around $650 billion on data center capital expenditures .\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e82; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e205 [third_party_view; Keithen Drury, fool.com; original source AI hyperscalers (capex plans; 2027 forecast is the author's) ; from gnews:d0f3f83296df884fae64921e 2026-07-07] Fool.com's Keithen Drury writes that the AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year (calendar 2026), and that next year (calendar 2027) that figure could be over $1 trillion.\\n    Quote: While the AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year, next year that figure could be over $1 trillion.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e83; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e215 [fact; 24/7 Wall St.; original source Google ; from gnews:295fceb25273abae9bb6c995 2026-07-07] Google reported that Google Cloud revenue rose 63% with backlog nearly doubling to over $460 billion, and guided 2026 capital expenditure up to $190 billion.\\n    Quote: Google Cloud revenue rose 63% with backlog nearly doubling to over $460 billion, and 2026 capex is guided up tp $190 billion.\",\n   \"- e250 [fact; Bloomberg (reported by Futuriom); original source Meta 2026-07-01; from gnews:cb05b9f49943ef0ba412cf36 2026-07-08] Bloomberg reported (July 1) that Meta is setting up a cloud-based business selling AI compute and models, on the same day NVIDIA announced its new revenue-sharing model; the article notes Meta had planned to buy 'millions' of GPUs from NVIDIA earlier this year and questions what its move implies for AI demand.\\n    Quote: On July 1, the same day NVIDIA announced its new revenue-sharing model, Bloomberg reported that Meta is setting up a cloud-based business selling AI compute and models. If Meta, which planned to buy “millions” of GPUs from NVIDIA earlier this year, now doesn’t need those chips, what does that say about AI demand?\",\n   \"- e256 [fact; 247wallst.com (Gerelyn Terzo); original source IES Holdings ; from gnews:abfc64ce8fc333f13d06a757 2026-07-08] IES Holdings, a data-center electrical and technology systems contractor, reported March-quarter 2026 results: Communications segment revenue rose 35% year over year to $367.7 million, which it attributed to continued strong demand in the data center market; Infrastructure Solutions revenue jumped 64% to $192.4 million; and total backlog rose 62% since the end of fiscal 2025 to $3.86 billion, driven by customer demand and expansion capacity.\\n    Quote: Communications revenue rose 35% year over year to $367.7 million, which the company attributed to “continued strong demand in the data center market,” adding that “the recent capital investments we have made have positioned us well to respond to that demand and deliver solutions to our customers.” … Infrastructure Solutions revenue jumped 64% to $192.4 million, and total backlog exploded to $3.86 \",\n   \"- e257 [fact; 247wallst.com (Gerelyn Terzo); original source Quanta Services ; from gnews:abfc64ce8fc333f13d06a757 2026-07-08] Quanta Services, a grid and electrical infrastructure contractor, reported Q1 2026 revenue of $7.87 billion, up 26.3% year over year, adjusted EPS of $2.68 versus $2.03 consensus, and backlog that rose to a record $48.5 billion; management raised full-year 2026 guidance to $34.70–35.20 billion in revenue and $13.55–14.25 in adjusted EPS, and cited a path to more than double adjusted EPS by 2030.\\n    Quote: Revenue hit $7.87 billion, up 26.3% year over year, adjusted EPS of $2.68 beat the $2.03 consensus by 31.88%, and backlog rocketed to a record $48.5 billion. Management raised full-year 2026 guidance to a range of $34.70 billion to $35.20 billion in revenue and $13.55 to $14.25 in adjusted EPS.\",\n   \"- e258 [fact; 247wallst.com (Gerelyn Terzo); original source Comfort Systems USA ; from gnews:abfc64ce8fc333f13d06a757 2026-07-08] Comfort Systems USA, a mechanical/HVAC contractor, reported Q1 2026 revenue of $2.87 billion, up 56.5% year over year (51% organic), diluted EPS of $10.51 versus $6.81 consensus, and backlog of $12.45 billion, nearly double the $6.89 billion a year earlier; data center and technology infrastructure now accounts for roughly 45% of company revenue.\\n    Quote: Q1 2026 revenue reached $2.87 billion, up 56.5% year over year, with organic growth of 51%. Diluted EPS of $10.51 obliterated the $6.81 consensus by 54.44%, and backlog climbed to $12.45 billion, nearly double the $6.89 billion of a year earlier. … Data center and technology infrastructure now accounts for roughly 45% of company revenue\",\n   \"- e259 [party_statement; 247wallst.com (Gerelyn Terzo); original source Comfort Systems USA (CFO Bill George) ; from gnews:abfc64ce8fc333f13d06a757 2026-07-08] On the Q1 2026 call, Comfort Systems USA finance chief Bill George said the constraint on the business is now a supply (labor) issue rather than demand, saying 'There is plenty more work we could take if we could possibly do it' — indicating data-center work demand exceeds the contractor's capacity to execute.\\n    Quote: Today, I think it’s really important for people to understand that it’s a supply issue. There is plenty more work we could take if we could possibly do it.\",\n   \"- e260 [fact; 247wallst.com (Gerelyn Terzo); original source EMCOR Group ; from gnews:abfc64ce8fc333f13d06a757 2026-07-08] EMCOR Group reported Q1 2026 diluted EPS of $6.84 versus $5.90 consensus, Remaining Performance Obligations at a record $15.62 billion, up 32.9% year over year, and raised full-year 2026 guidance to $18.50–19.25 billion in revenue with EPS of $28.25–29.75.\\n    Quote: Diluted EPS of $6.84 beat the $5.90 consensus by 15.85%, Remaining Performance Obligations hit a record $15.62 billion, up 32.9% year over year, and management raised full-year 2026 guidance to $18.50 billion to $19.25 billion in revenue with EPS of $28.25 to $29.75.\",\n   \"- e261 [fact; 247wallst.com (Gerelyn Terzo); original source Sterling Infrastructure ; from gnews:abfc64ce8fc333f13d06a757 2026-07-08] Sterling Infrastructure reported Q1 2026 EPS of $3.59 (beating consensus by a double-digit percentage), revenue up 91.59% to $825.7 million, and E-Infrastructure Solutions revenue up 174% year over year to $597.7 million at a 23.5% adjusted operating margin; it raised full-year guidance to adjusted diluted EPS of $18.40–19.05, implying 72% growth at the midpoint.\\n    Quote: Q1 2026 EPS of $3.59 crushed the consensus by a double-digit percentage, revenue jumped 91.59% to $825.7 million, and E-Infrastructure Solutions revenue rocketed 174% year over year to $597.7 million at a 23.5% adjusted operating margin. Management raised the full-year guidance to adjusted diluted EPS of $18.40 to $19.05, implying 72% growth at the midpoint.\",\n   \"- e353 [fact; Reuters (reported by gurufocus.com); original source Meta Platforms ; from gnews:09b9d76297a57f198651c5e7 2026-07-10] Reuters reported that Meta Platforms plans to begin manufacturing its custom AI processor, code-named Iris, in September after completing initial testing. The chip is being designed with Broadcom and manufactured by TSMC, and the report said it is intended to support Meta's AI infrastructure while complementing, rather than replacing, the large number of graphics processors Meta continues to buy from Nvidia and Advanced Micro Devices.\\n    Quote: Reuters said Meta plans to begin manufacturing its custom AI processor, code-named Iris, in September after completing initial testing. The chip is being designed with Broadcom (AVGO) and manufactured by Taiwan Semiconductor Manufacturing (TSM). The report said the processor is intended to support Meta's AI infrastructure while complementing, rather than replacing, the large number of graphics pro\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e336; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e445 [fact; Alex Sirois, 247wallst.com; original source Broadcom ; from gnews:799ec454c15ddf61f08d042f 2026-07-13] Broadcom reported Q2 FY2026 revenue of $22.187 billion, up 47.9%, including AI semiconductor sales of $10.80 billion that grew 143%, as reported by 247wallst.com analyst Alex Sirois.\\n    Quote: Broadcom’s Q2 FY2026 revenue hit $22.187 billion, up 47.9%, with AI semiconductor sales of $10.80 billion growing 143%.\",\n   \"- e449 [party_statement; Alex Sirois, 247wallst.com; original source Broadcom ; from gnews:799ec454c15ddf61f08d042f 2026-07-13] Broadcom management has telegraphed $16.0 billion of AI revenue for its Q3, roughly 54% of total sales, ahead of its Q3 report scheduled for September 8, according to the article.\\n    Quote: Broadcom’s Q3 earnings report lands September 8, and management has already telegraphed $16.0 billion in AI revenue, roughly 54% of total sales.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e136; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e467 [party_statement; 24/7 Wall St. (Alex Sirois); original source Meta Platforms ; from gnews:221f52333c8f5e936e515c4d 2026-07-13] Meta Platforms raised its 2026 capital expenditure plan to $125-$145 billion, the article reports.\\n    Quote: Meta raised 2026 capex to $125 to $145 billion\",\n   \"- e445 [fact; Alex Sirois, 247wallst.com; original source Broadcom ; from gnews:799ec454c15ddf61f08d042f 2026-07-13] Broadcom reported Q2 FY2026 revenue of $22.187 billion, up 47.9%, including AI semiconductor sales of $10.80 billion that grew 143%, as reported by 247wallst.com analyst Alex Sirois.\\n    Quote: Broadcom’s Q2 FY2026 revenue hit $22.187 billion, up 47.9%, with AI semiconductor sales of $10.80 billion growing 143%.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e489; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e475 [party_statement; Justin Pope, The Motley Fool; original source Meta Platforms, Microsoft, Alphabet and Amazon (the big four hyperscalers) ; from gnews:84e90d3da2d3779da0e7bdb0 2026-07-14] The article reports that the big four AI hyperscalers — Meta Platforms, Microsoft, Alphabet and Amazon — are planning higher capital expenditures in 2026. No individual capex figures are given. Reported by Justin Pope of The Motley Fool.\\n    Quote: Hyperscalers, including Meta Platforms , Microsoft , Alphabet , and Amazon , are planning higher capital expenditures in 2026.\",\n   \"- e476 [third_party_view; Justin Pope, The Motley Fool; original source Goldman Sachs ; from gnews:84e90d3da2d3779da0e7bdb0 2026-07-14] Goldman Sachs estimates that industry AI compute spending will grow from approximately $494 billion in 2026 to $1.13 trillion by 2031, as reported by The Motley Fool's Justin Pope. This is a broader industry demand forecast, not a figure for NVIDIA or for the big four's data-center capex specifically.\\n    Quote: Goldman Sachs estimates that AI compute spending will grow from approximately $494 billion this year to $1.13 trillion by 2031.\",\n   \"- e496 [party_statement; Vandita Jadeja (247wallst.com); original source Meta ; from gnews:1e1fa5a194198eaf05d90c9e 2026-07-14] Meta's $145 billion infrastructure budget, aimed partly at custom silicon, was cited among competitive threats facing NVIDIA.\\n    Quote: The Reddit crowd has fixated on competitive threats, from DeepSeek’s rumored in-house chip to Meta’s $145B infrastructure budget aimed partly at custom silicon.\",\n   \"- e514 [party_statement; 247wallst.com (Vandita Jadeja); original source Broadcom (Hock Tan, CEO) ; from gnews:386a294bd395bde25e3f4a11 2026-07-14] Broadcom CEO Hock Tan said momentum continues, with fiscal Q3 AI semiconductor revenue set to grow more than 200% year over year to $16 billion; Broadcom guided total Q3 revenue to $29.4 billion (+84% YoY), set a stated goal of exceeding $100 billion in AI sales by 2027, and reported AI semiconductor revenue of $10.8 billion last quarter, up 143% YoY — custom AI silicon revenue that competes for the same AI data-center spending as NVIDIA, per 247wallst.com's 2026-07-14 article.\\n    Quote: Broadcom’s Hock Tan says “the momentum continues” with Q3 AI semi revenue set to grow over 200% year-over-year to $16 billion. … Broadcom guided Q3 revenue to $29.4B (+84% YoY), and management’s stated goal is to exceed $100B in AI sales by 2027. AI semi revenue grew 143% YoY to $10.8B last quarter.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e136; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e535 [fact; EBC (ebc.com); original source Amazon.com Inc. ; from gnews:f83ae0a4f01c18d48f20a629 2026-07-14] Amazon recently borrowed another $25 billion, causing a minor selloff in AI-related debt, as investors question hyperscaler returns on their large AI hardware spending, EBC reported.\\n    Quote: Amazon recently made headlines by borrowing another $25 billion, causing a minor selloff in AI-related debt . … These tech behemoths have spent hundreds of billions of dollars purchasing Nvidia's hardware, but Wall Street is starting to demand proof of return on investment (ROI).\"\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"control\": 24,\n  \"ruler_question\": {\n   \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA begin shipping its Rubin Ultra generation, in Kyber NVL144 racks, by December 31, 2027?\",\n   \"unit\": \"yes/no\",\n   \"due_day\": \"2027-12-31\",\n   \"rungs\": [\n    {\n     \"label\": \"Yes\",\n     \"condition\": \"NVIDIA has begun shipping Rubin Ultra systems by December 31, 2027.\",\n     \"lo\": null,\n     \"hi\": null\n    },\n    {\n     \"label\": \"No\",\n     \"condition\": \"NVIDIA has not begun shipping Rubin Ultra systems by December 31, 2027.\",\n     \"lo\": null,\n     \"hi\": null\n    }\n   ]\n  },\n  \"judged_question\": {\n   \"view_id\": \"v7\",\n   \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA confirm that first customer shipments of its Vera Rubin Ultra Kyber NVL144 rack-scale system have begun by December 31, 2028?\",\n   \"looks_at\": \"First customer shipments of the Kyber rack-scale architecture (NVL144) with Vera Rubin Ultra, confirmed by NVIDIA.\",\n   \"due_day\": \"2028-12-31\",\n   \"year\": \"2028\",\n   \"kind\": \"asset\"\n  },\n  \"judged_rungs\": [\n   {\n    \"position\": 1,\n    \"label\": \"Yes\",\n    \"condition\": \"By December 31, 2028, NVIDIA confirms that first customer shipments of the Vera Rubin Ultra Kyber NVL144 rack-scale system have begun.\",\n    \"lo\": null,\n    \"hi\": null\n   },\n   {\n    \"position\": 2,\n    \"label\": \"Ruled out\",\n    \"condition\": \"After July 5, 2026 and before December 31, 2028, NVIDIA formally cancels the Vera Rubin Ultra Kyber NVL144 system or abandons plans to ship it to customers. This rung holds when that, or any other decision against the event of rung 1 as a whole, or a formal abandonment or withdrawal of it as a whole, was made after 2026-07-05 and stands on 2028-12-31, and only when rung 1 does not.\",\n    \"lo\": null,\n    \"hi\": null\n   },\n   {\n    \"position\": 3,\n    \"label\": \"Still open\",\n    \"condition\": \"Neither rung 1 nor rung 2 holds on 2028-12-31.\",\n    \"lo\": null,\n    \"hi\": null\n   }\n  ],\n  \"reader_b_decisive_items\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"i100\",\n    \"day\": \"2026-07-05\",\n    \"title\": \"Nvidia's next-gen AI rack system delayed to 2028 on manufacturing snags, SemiAnalysis says\",\n    \"passage\": \"Kyber NVL144 rack architecture has been delayed to 2028 as the PCB midplane remains challenging from a manufacturability standpoint\",\n    \"meaning\": {\n     \"who\": \"SemiAnalysis (a research firm's report, not NVIDIA)\",\n     \"what\": \"Kyber NVL144 rack for Vera Rubin Ultra delayed; cause: PCB midplane manufacturability\",\n     \"how_much\": \"more than 12 months\",\n     \"period\": \"from 2027 to 2028\",\n     \"status\": \"claim\"\n    }\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"i179\",\n    \"day\": \"2026-07-06\",\n    \"title\": \"Nvidia's Kyber NVL144 reportedly pushed back more than a year, Asian suppliers drop\",\n    \"passage\": \"The more powerful version of the upcoming Rubin Ultra chip with four compute dies has also been canceled. Only the smaller two-die version remains, delivering roughly half the real-world compute performance.\",\n    \"meaning\": {\n     \"who\": \"SemiAnalysis\",\n     \"what\": \"four-die Rubin Ultra cancelled; two-die version remains\",\n     \"how_much\": \"roughly half the real-world compute\",\n     \"status\": \"claim\",\n     \"status_note\": \"cancelled\"\n    }\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"i181\",\n    \"day\": \"2026-07-06\",\n    \"title\": \"Nvidia's Kyber NVL144 reportedly pushed back more than a year, Asian suppliers drop\",\n    \"passage\": \"Nvidia plans to make up for the shortfall by selling more Oberon-Rubin racks in the existing form factor.\",\n    \"meaning\": {\n     \"who\": \"SemiAnalysis, about NVIDIA's plan\",\n     \"what\": \"sell more Oberon-Rubin racks to cover the Kyber gap\",\n     \"status\": \"plan\",\n     \"status_note\": \"as claimed\"\n    }\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"i101\",\n    \"day\": \"2026-07-05\",\n    \"title\": \"Nvidia's next-gen AI rack system delayed to 2028 on manufacturing snags, SemiAnalysis says\",\n    \"passage\": \"Our roadmap is intact\",\n    \"meaning\": {\n     \"who\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"what\": \"rejects the SemiAnalysis report; says its roadmap (Rubin Ultra / Kyber in 2027) is unchanged\",\n     \"status\": \"claim\"\n    }\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"i197\",\n    \"day\": \"2026-07-06\",\n    \"title\": \"Nvidia's Kyber rack for Rubin Ultra reportedly delayed to 2028, stopgap solution also axed due to customer pushback — Analyst firm SemiAnalysis says PCB midplane problems led to the delay [Updated]\",\n    \"passage\": \"An Nvidia representative responded with a short statement to Tom's Hardware : \\\"Our roadmap is intact.\\\"\",\n    \"meaning\": {\n     \"who\": \"NVIDIA representative\",\n     \"what\": \"denies the report: roadmap intact\",\n     \"condition\": \"unclear whether the original or an already updated roadmap is meant\",\n     \"status\": \"claim\"\n    }\n   }\n  ],\n  \"evidence_shown_to_the_judge_through_2026_07_14\": [\n   \"- e90 [third_party_view; CNBC; original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:552f8c2bdfd080555d592587 2026-07-05] Research firm SemiAnalysis reported that NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the server cabinet designed to pack 144 GPUs and slated to debut with NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin Ultra system in 2027 — has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028, because the multi-layer PCB midplane at the heart of the system 'remains challenging from a manufacturability standpoint.' SemiAnalysis also said the larger NVL576 system, which links eight racks via optical connections, is likely delayed or limited to small volumes.\\n    Quote: Nvidia 's next marquee product — the Kyber rack-scale architecture designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips — has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028, according to research firm SemiAnalysis, the latest in a string of reported setbacks raising questions about the AI giant's product roadmap. … The setback stems from difficulties manufacturing a key circuit board at the heart of the syst\",\n   \"- e91 [third_party_view; CNBC; original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:552f8c2bdfd080555d592587 2026-07-05] SemiAnalysis reported that NVIDIA's backup plan — bolting two of its current-generation racks together to achieve similar power — has been scrapped after cloud service providers and hyperscalers pushed back on the design's 'odd design and heavy operational burden.' SemiAnalysis said this leaves NVIDIA with 'no proven solution to expand the scale-up world size for Rubin Ultra,' which it predicted could give AMD and Google a rare technical opening at the high end of the market.\\n    Quote: A backup plan — bolting two of Nvidia's current-generation racks together for similar power — has also been scrapped after cloud customers rejected the design as awkward and costly to operate. … \\\"It has since been cancelled due to heavy pushback from CSPs [cloud service providers] and hyperscalers over its odd design and heavy operational burden,\\\" SemiAnalysis said. … That leaves Nvidia with \\\"no p\",\n   \"- e92 [party_statement; CNBC; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:552f8c2bdfd080555d592587 2026-07-05] NVIDIA rejected the SemiAnalysis report that its Kyber/Rubin Ultra roadmap had slipped, stating 'Our roadmap is intact.'\\n    Quote: Nvidia rejected the SemiAnalysis report and said, \\\"Our roadmap is intact.\\\"\",\n   \"- e90 [third_party_view; CNBC; original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:552f8c2bdfd080555d592587 2026-07-05] Research firm SemiAnalysis reported that NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the server cabinet designed to pack 144 GPUs and slated to debut with NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin Ultra system in 2027 — has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028, because the multi-layer PCB midplane at the heart of the system 'remains challenging from a manufacturability standpoint.' SemiAnalysis also said the larger NVL576 system, which links eight racks via optical connections, is likely delayed or limited to small volumes.\\n    Quote: Nvidia 's next marquee product — the Kyber rack-scale architecture designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips — has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028, according to research firm SemiAnalysis, the latest in a string of reported setbacks raising questions about the AI giant's product roadmap. … The setback stems from difficulties manufacturing a key circuit board at the heart of the syst\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e106, e122, e164, e167, e171, e175, e177; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e91 [third_party_view; CNBC; original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:552f8c2bdfd080555d592587 2026-07-05] SemiAnalysis reported that NVIDIA's backup plan — bolting two of its current-generation racks together to achieve similar power — has been scrapped after cloud service providers and hyperscalers pushed back on the design's 'odd design and heavy operational burden.' SemiAnalysis said this leaves NVIDIA with 'no proven solution to expand the scale-up world size for Rubin Ultra,' which it predicted could give AMD and Google a rare technical opening at the high end of the market.\\n    Quote: A backup plan — bolting two of Nvidia's current-generation racks together for similar power — has also been scrapped after cloud customers rejected the design as awkward and costly to operate. … \\\"It has since been cancelled due to heavy pushback from CSPs [cloud service providers] and hyperscalers over its odd design and heavy operational burden,\\\" SemiAnalysis said. … That leaves Nvidia with \\\"no p\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e107, e165, e169, e178; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e92 [party_statement; CNBC; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:552f8c2bdfd080555d592587 2026-07-05] NVIDIA rejected the SemiAnalysis report that its Kyber/Rubin Ultra roadmap had slipped, stating 'Our roadmap is intact.'\\n    Quote: Nvidia rejected the SemiAnalysis report and said, \\\"Our roadmap is intact.\\\"\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e108, e124, e168, e180; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e106 [third_party_view; Lockridge Okoth, beincrypto.com (via finance.yahoo.com); original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:68a3ed21f77b935d9fa7ec5e 2026-07-06] Research firm SemiAnalysis claimed that NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 high-density rack architecture, built for Rubin Ultra GPUs and showcased at GTC earlier this year, has slipped by more than 12 months, blaming persistent manufacturing problems with the system's complex PCB midplane.\\n    Quote: SemiAnalysis claims the high-density rack design built for Rubin Ultra GPUs has slipped by more than 12 months. The firm blamed persistent manufacturing problems with the system's complex PCB midplane.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e90; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e108 [party_statement; Lockridge Okoth, beincrypto.com (via finance.yahoo.com); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:68a3ed21f77b935d9fa7ec5e 2026-07-06] NVIDIA rejected SemiAnalysis's delay claims, telling media outlets that its roadmap remains intact.\\n    Quote: Nvidia rejected the claims, telling media outlets that its roadmap remains intact.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e92; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read it as a later version of e91 (); both are in force: SemiAnalysis reported that NVIDIA's backup plan — bolting two of its current-generation racks together to achieve similar power — has been scrapped after cloud service providers and hyperscalers pushed back on the design's 'odd design and heavy operational burden.' SemiAnalysis said this leaves NVIDIA with 'no proven solution to expand the scale-up world size for Rubin Ultra,' which it predicted could give AMD and Google a rare technical opening at the high end of the market.\",\n   \"- e122 [third_party_view; DataCenterDynamics (Charlotte Trueman), reporting SemiAnalysis's claims; original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:98a4e8ff0c0260d8dc9e531d 2026-07-06] SemiAnalysis reported (in an item published 2026-07-06) that Nvidia has delayed production of its Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture by more than 12 months, pushing its release to 2028 from an original 2027 target, because of manufacturing challenges with the printed circuit board (PCB) midplane that connects the modules. SemiAnalysis also said Nvidia's alternative plan — placing two Oberon racks back-to-back to achieve similar power output — was scrapped after cloud companies and hyperscalers pushed back, and that the same manufacturing issues will likely also delay the planned NVL576 system or limit it to small volumes.\\n    Quote: Nvidia has delayed production of its Kyber rack-scale architecture by more than 12 months, pushing its release date to 2028, SemiAnalysis has reported. … The Kyber server cabinets, which have been designed to house 144 Rubin Ultra GPUs connected via the company’s NVLink 7 Switch, had originally been slated for release in 2027. … However, the research firm claimed that issues in manufacturing the p\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e90; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e123 [third_party_view; DataCenterDynamics (Charlotte Trueman), reporting SemiAnalysis's claims; original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:98a4e8ff0c0260d8dc9e531d 2026-07-06] SemiAnalysis separately reported, about a week before the 2026-07-06 DCD article, that Nvidia had canceled the four-die Rubin Ultra chip design in favor of a two-chiplet design, citing manufacturing execution concerns; per SemiAnalysis, the two-die offering will deliver around half the performance of the four-die Rubin Ultra.\\n    Quote: The news comes a week after SemiAnalysis separately reported that Nvidia had canceled the four-die Rubin Ultra design in favor of the two-chiplet design, also due to “manufacturing execution concerns.” The two-die offering will deliver around half the performance of the four-die Rubin Ultra.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e181; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e124 [party_statement; DataCenterDynamics (Charlotte Trueman); original source Nvidia 2026-07-06; from gnews:98a4e8ff0c0260d8dc9e531d 2026-07-06] In response to a DataCenterDynamics request for comment on SemiAnalysis's Kyber claims, an Nvidia spokesperson said: \\\"Our roadmap is intact.\\\"\\n    Quote: In response to a request for comment from DCD regarding the Kyber claims, a spokesperson for Nvidia said: \\\"Our roadmap is intact.\\\"\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e92; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e134 [rumour; 247wallst.com (Danielle Liverance); original source  ; from gnews:3df0bbc8aff2c1d008bc0a32 2026-07-06] NVIDIA shares were reported to be under pressure on reports of delays across its upcoming lineup of Vera Rubin server systems; the reports are unattributed in the article.\\n    Quote: NVIDIA shares are under pressure on reports of delays across their upcoming lineup of Vera Rubin server systems.\",\n   \"- e164 [third_party_view; the-decoder.com (reporting SemiAnalysis's post on X); original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:795ea94e1100415a67fd1a96 2026-07-06] Analyst firm SemiAnalysis reported on X that Nvidia's next-generation AI server rack system, Kyber NVL144, has been pushed back more than twelve months to 2028 because of manufacturing problems, specifically the PCB midplane — a central circuit board connecting all the individual components that has proven extremely difficult to produce without defects.\\n    Quote: Asian tech stocks slid after analyst firm SemiAnalysis reported on X that Nvidia's next AI server rack system, Kyber NVL144, has been pushed back more than twelve months to 2028 because of manufacturing problems. … The issue, according to SemiAnalysis, is the circuit boards. Specifically, the PCB midplane, a central board that connects all the individual components, has proven extremely difficult \\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e90; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e165 [third_party_view; the-decoder.com (reporting SemiAnalysis); original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:795ea94e1100415a67fd1a96 2026-07-06] SemiAnalysis reported additional setbacks to Nvidia's roadmap: a planned alternative design, NVL72x2 — which would have placed two Oberon racks back to back — has been scrapped entirely after cloud providers and large data-center operators pushed back on its unusual form factor and high operational overhead; and the more powerful version of the upcoming Rubin Ultra chip with four compute dies has been canceled, leaving only the smaller two-die version that delivers roughly half the real-world compute performance.\\n    Quote: SemiAnalysis lists more setbacks. A planned alternative design called NVL72x2, which would have placed two Oberon racks back to back, has been scrapped entirely. Cloud providers and large data center operators pushed back against the unusual form factor and the high operational overhead. … The more powerful version of the upcoming Rubin Ultra chip with four compute dies has also been canceled. Onl\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e91; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e166 [third_party_view; the-decoder.com (reporting SemiAnalysis); original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:795ea94e1100415a67fd1a96 2026-07-06] SemiAnalysis reported that Nvidia's key interconnect technology CPO-NVSwitch, which links many chips into a single large system, will not arrive until the generation after next, called Feynman, meaning Nvidia lacks a proven way to scale Rubin Ultra to very large systems for now; the report says the gap could give competitors like AMD's MI500X or Google's TPUv8i Broadfly room to move in, and Nvidia plans to make up for the shortfall by selling more Oberon-Rubin racks in the existing form factor.\\n    Quote: A key interconnect technology called CPO-NVSwitch, which links many chips into a single large system, won't arrive until the generation after next, called Feynman. That means Nvidia lacks a proven way to scale Rubin Ultra to very large systems for now. The gap could give competitors like AMD's MI500X or Google's TPUv8i Broadfly room to move in. Nvidia plans to make up for the shortfall by selling \",\n   \"- e167 [rumour; Investing.com (uk.finance.yahoo.com); original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:d5c48abb470f394f86549b6b 2026-07-06] Supply-chain research firm SemiAnalysis claimed in a weekend report (reported 2026-07-06) that Nvidia's Kyber NVL144 rack architecture will be delayed to 2028 because of manufacturing challenges with its PCB midplane, a claim Nvidia disputed.\\n    Quote: Supply chain research firm SemiAnalysis claimed in a weekend report that Nvidia's highly anticipated Kyber NVL144 rack architecture will be delayed to 2028 due to manufacturing challenges with its PCB midplane.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e90; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e168 [party_statement; NVIDIA spokesperson, via Investing.com (uk.finance.yahoo.com); original source NVIDIA Corporation ; from gnews:d5c48abb470f394f86549b6b 2026-07-06] An Nvidia spokesperson, in a brief statement emailed to Investing.com, refuted SemiAnalysis's claims that its next-generation rack architectures are delayed, saying: \\\"Our roadmap is intact.\\\"\\n    Quote: An Nvidia spokesperson swiftly refuted such claims in a brief statement emailed to Investing.com, stating: \\\"Our roadmap is intact.\\\"\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e92; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read it as a later version of e166 (); both are in force: SemiAnalysis reported that Nvidia's key interconnect technology CPO-NVSwitch, which links many chips into a single large system, will not arrive until the generation after next, called Feynman, meaning Nvidia lacks a proven way to scale Rubin Ultra to very large systems for now; the report says the gap could give competitors like AMD's MI500X or Google's TPUv8i Broadfly room to move in, and Nvidia plans to make up for the shortfall by selling more Oberon-Rubin racks in the existing form factor.\",\n   \"- e169 [rumour; Investing.com (uk.finance.yahoo.com); original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:d5c48abb470f394f86549b6b 2026-07-06] SemiAnalysis's report also alleged that Nvidia cancelled an alternative back-to-back rack design after heavy operational pushback from hyperscale cloud clients, and that a crucial four-die configuration of a downsized version of the upcoming Rubin Ultra platform had been scrapped.\\n    Quote: The research firm further alleged that Nvidia cancelled an alternative back-to-back rack design following heavy operational pushback from hyperscale cloud clients. … Additionally, the report pointed to a downsized version of the upcoming Rubin Ultra platform, claiming a crucial four-die configuration had been scrapped.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e91; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e171 [third_party_view; tikr.com (TIKR); original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:d4c5dc540392f8df39aee250 2026-07-06] Research firm SemiAnalysis reported that Nvidia's next rack-scale server design, Kyber — built to pack 144 Nvidia chips into a single cabinet — has been pushed back more than a year, from 2027 to 2028, due to manufacturing problems with a specialized circuit board. SemiAnalysis also reported that the larger NVL576 version (eight racks linked optically) is likely to be delayed or ship only in small volumes, and that Nvidia's backup plan of bolting two current-generation racks together was scrapped after cloud customers reportedly rejected it as awkward and expensive to run. SemiAnalysis said this leaves Nvidia without a proven way to scale up Rubin Ultra for now, potentially giving rivals like AMD and Google an opening at the high end of the AI chip market.\\n    Quote: Research firm SemiAnalysis reported that Kyber, Nvidia’s next rack-scale server design, has been pushed back more than a year , from 2027 to 2028. … The design was supposed to launch alongside Vera Rubin Ultra, Nvidia’s next-generation system, in 2027. The holdup comes down to manufacturing problems with a specialized circuit board inside the system, according to SemiAnalysis. … A larger version, \\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e90; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e175 [third_party_view; Bloomberg / businesstimes.com.sg; original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:c0432377817a1c00a1bbadf0 2026-07-06] Research firm SemiAnalysis said in an X post that Nvidia's next-generation Kyber NVL144 AI server rack system has been delayed by more than a year due to manufacturing difficulties, specifically setbacks in the construction of printed circuit boards for the platform. Nvidia did not respond to a request for comment. The report was unconfirmed by Nvidia.\\n    Quote: Asian technology stocks slumped after a report that Nvidia’s next-generation AI server rack system has been delayed by more than a year due to manufacturing difficulties. … Research firm SemiAnalysis said in an X post that Nvidia’s Kyber NVL144 hit setbacks in the construction of printed circuit boards for the platform. Nvidia did not respond to a request for comment outside of regular office hour\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e90; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e177 [rumour; Tom's Hardware (Luke James), reporting a SemiAnalysis thread on X; original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:c75f16a6d64cc27fc28fb571 2026-07-06] According to SemiAnalysis, in a thread on X reported by Tom's Hardware, NVIDIA will not ship its Kyber NVL144 rack until 2028 — a delay of more than 12 months — pushing the cabinet intended for 2027's Rubin Ultra GPUs into the following year. The report attributes the holdup to manufacturing challenges with the PCB midplane (which NVIDIA calls the orthogonal backplane) that connects eight Oberon racks between the NVSwitches.\\n    Quote: Nvidia reportedly won't ship its Kyber NVL144 rack until 2028, a delay of more than 12 months that pushes the cabinet meant for 2027's Rubin Ultra GPUs into the following year, according to a SemiAnalysis thread on X. The holdup is ostensibly being caused by manufacturing challenges with a PCB midplane that connects eight Oberon racks between the NVSwitches, which Nvidia calls the orthogonal backp\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e90; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e178 [rumour; Tom's Hardware (Luke James), reporting SemiAnalysis; original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:c75f16a6d64cc27fc28fb571 2026-07-06] SemiAnalysis reported that NVIDIA killed NVL72x2, a stopgap rack that would have bolted two Oberon racks back-to-back to reach Kyber-class density over copper NVLink, after its largest customers balked at running two linked cabinets as a single unit. The report says no proven alternative remains to widen Rubin Ultra's scale-up in 2027.\\n    Quote: Nvidia is also understood to have killed NVL72x2, a stopgap rack designed to tide customers over, and that no proven alternative is now available to widen Rubin Ultra's scale-up in 2027. … NVL72x2 would have bolted two Oberon racks back-to-back to reach Kyber-class density over copper NVLink, per SemiAnalysis , which said Nvidia abandoned the stopgap after its largest customers balked at running t\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e91; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e179 [third_party_view; Tom's Hardware (Luke James), reporting SemiAnalysis; original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:c75f16a6d64cc27fc28fb571 2026-07-06] SemiAnalysis said NVL576 — a separate configuration tying eight racks together through co-packaged optics — is likely to slip too, or to ship in low volume, until that optical technology matures.\\n    Quote: NVL576, a separate configuration tying eight racks together through co-packaged optics, is likely to slip too or ship in low volume until that optical technology matures.\",\n   \"- e180 [party_statement; Tom's Hardware (Luke James); original source NVIDIA 2026-07-06; from gnews:c75f16a6d64cc27fc28fb571 2026-07-06] An NVIDIA representative told Tom's Hardware, in a statement dated 7/6/2026, that \\\"Our roadmap is intact,\\\" providing no further details in response to follow-up questions about the reported Kyber delay.\\n    Quote: Update 7/6/2026 4:10pm PT : An Nvidia representative responded with a short statement to Tom's Hardware : \\\"Our roadmap is intact.\\\" Nvidia provided no further details in response to our follow-up questions.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e92; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e181 [rumour; Tom's Hardware (Luke James), reporting SemiAnalysis; original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:c75f16a6d64cc27fc28fb571 2026-07-06] According to the report, NVIDIA dropped the quad-chiplet Rubin Ultra GPU for a dual-chiplet part about a week before the 7/6/2026 article, over manufacturing execution concerns, halving the accelerator's per-package compute.\\n    Quote: Nvidia dropped the quad-chiplet Rubin Ultra GPU for a dual-chiplet part last week over manufacturing execution concerns, halving the accelerator's per-package compute.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e123; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e182 [third_party_view; Tom's Hardware (Luke James); original source  ; from gnews:c75f16a6d64cc27fc28fb571 2026-07-06] Tom's Hardware reported that the delay applies only to the Rubin Ultra phase and its Kyber rack; NVIDIA's 2026 Rubin GPUs, which reuse the current Oberon rack, are not part of the reported delay.\\n    Quote: The delay only applies only to the Rubin Ultra phase and its Kyber rack. Nvidia's 2026 Rubin GPUs, which reuse the current Oberon rack, aren't part of the reported delay.\",\n   \"- e90 [third_party_view; CNBC; original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:552f8c2bdfd080555d592587 2026-07-05] Research firm SemiAnalysis reported that NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the server cabinet designed to pack 144 GPUs and slated to debut with NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin Ultra system in 2027 — has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028, because the multi-layer PCB midplane at the heart of the system 'remains challenging from a manufacturability standpoint.' SemiAnalysis also said the larger NVL576 system, which links eight racks via optical connections, is likely delayed or limited to small volumes.\\n    Quote: Nvidia 's next marquee product — the Kyber rack-scale architecture designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips — has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028, according to research firm SemiAnalysis, the latest in a string of reported setbacks raising questions about the AI giant's product roadmap. … The setback stems from difficulties manufacturing a key circuit board at the heart of the syst\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e106, e122, e164, e167, e171, e175, e177, e194, e210; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e92 [party_statement; CNBC; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:552f8c2bdfd080555d592587 2026-07-05] NVIDIA rejected the SemiAnalysis report that its Kyber/Rubin Ultra roadmap had slipped, stating 'Our roadmap is intact.'\\n    Quote: Nvidia rejected the SemiAnalysis report and said, \\\"Our roadmap is intact.\\\"\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e108, e124, e168, e180, e195, e211; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e194 [third_party_view; CNBC; original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:b48593184704bbbe6eddbc03 2026-07-07] A research report, identified later in the CNBC article as a SemiAnalysis report, claimed that Nvidia was at least a year behind on manufacturing of its next line of server racks (the article refers to the next-gen Kyber server rack running into delays). CNBC reported the claim as the backdrop to Nvidia's share performance a day after the report; the item gives no specific dates, volumes or shipment figures for the delay itself.\\n    Quote: Shares of Nvidia fought into the green despite a 5% sell-off in chip stocks tracked by the VanEck Semiconductor ETF (SMH) , a notable outperformance a day after a research report claiming the AI leader was at least a year behind on manufacturing of its next line of server racks. … Similar action took place in Nvidia Monday after the company disputed a report by SemiAnalysis that its next-gen Kyber\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e90; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e195 [party_statement; CNBC; original source NVIDIA 2026-07-06; from gnews:b48593184704bbbe6eddbc03 2026-07-07] Nvidia disputed a SemiAnalysis report that its next-gen Kyber server rack was running into delays, according to CNBC; the item reports the dispute occurred on the Monday before the 2026-07-07 article and does not quote Nvidia's statement or give any revised schedule. Nvidia's denial is the company's own assertion that