im (2027)\",\n        \"what\": \"data-center capital expenditure of the big four\",\n        \"how_much\": \"around $650bn (2026); more than $1 trillion (2027)\",\n        \"period\": \"calendar 2026; calendar 2027\",\n        \"status\": \"plan (2026); forecast by NVIDIA (2027)\"\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"i232\",\n      \"day\": \"2026-07-07\",\n      \"title\": \"A Circuit Board Problem Just Delayed Nvidia's Next AI System to 2028 And Chip Stocks Are Already Feeling the Fallout\",\n      \"passage\": \"2026 capex is guided up tp $190 billion\",\n      \"meaning\": {\n        \"who\": \"Alphabet / Google\",\n        \"what\": \"capital expenditure guidance\",\n        \"how_much\": \"up to $190bn\",\n        \"period\": \"2026\",\n        \"status\": \"forecast (guidance)\"\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"i233\",\n      \"day\": \"2026-07-07\",\n      \"title\": \"A Circuit Board Problem Just Delayed Nvidia's Next AI System to 2028 And Chip Stocks Are Already Feeling the Fallout\",\n      \"passage\": \"Google Cloud revenue rose 63% with backlog nearly doubling to over $460 billion\",\n      \"meaning\": {\n        \"who\": \"Google Cloud\",\n        \"what\": \"revenue growth and backlog\",\n        \"how_much\": \"revenue +63%; backlog nearly doubled to over $460bn\",\n        \"period\": \"latest reported quarter\",\n        \"status\": \"happened\"\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"i295\",\n      \"day\": \"2026-07-10\",\n      \"title\": \"Here are Friday's biggest analyst calls: Nvidia, SpaceX, Toll, Shopify, Twilio, Seagate, Chipotle & more\",\n      \"passage\": \"the company describing \\\"accelerating growth rates\\\" even as revenues approach the $100 bn per quarter mark\",\n      \"meaning\": {\n        \"who\": \"NVIDIA management, relayed by Morgan Stanley\",\n        \"what\": \"revenue growth rates accelerating\",\n        \"how_much\": \"quarterly revenue approaching $100 bn\",\n        \"status\": \"claim\"\n      }\n    }\n  ]\n}\nEVIDENCE COUNT 31\n{\n  \"control\": 24,\n  \"ruler_question\": {\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA begin shipping its Rubin Ultra generation, in Kyber NVL144 racks, by December 31, 2027?\",\n    \"unit\": \"yes/no\",\n    \"due_day\": \"2027-12-31\",\n    \"rungs\": [\n      {\n        \"label\": \"Yes\",\n        \"condition\": \"NVIDIA has begun shipping Rubin Ultra systems by December 31, 2027.\",\n        \"lo\": null,\n        \"hi\": null\n      },\n      {\n        \"label\": \"No\",\n        \"condition\": \"NVIDIA has not begun shipping Rubin Ultra systems by December 31, 2027.\",\n        \"lo\": null,\n        \"hi\": null\n      }\n    ]\n  },\n  \"judged_question\": {\n    \"view_id\": \"v7\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA confirm that first customer shipments of its Vera Rubin Ultra Kyber NVL144 rack-scale system have begun by December 31, 2028?\",\n    \"looks_at\": \"First customer shipments of the Kyber rack-scale architecture (NVL144) with Vera Rubin Ultra, confirmed by NVIDIA.\",\n    \"due_day\": \"2028-12-31\",\n    \"year\": \"2028\",\n    \"kind\": \"asset\"\n  },\n  \"judged_rungs\": [\n    {\n      \"position\": 1,\n      \"label\": \"Yes\",\n      \"condition\": \"By December 31, 2028, NVIDIA confirms that first customer shipments of the Vera Rubin Ultra Kyber NVL144 rack-scale system have begun.\",\n      \"lo\": null,\n      \"hi\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"position\": 2,\n      \"label\": \"Ruled out\",\n      \"condition\": \"After July 5, 2026 and before December 31, 2028, NVIDIA formally cancels the Vera Rubin Ultra Kyber NVL144 system or abandons plans to ship it to customers. This rung holds when that, or any other decision against the event of rung 1 as a whole, or a formal abandonment or withdrawal of it as a whole, was made after 2026-07-05 and stands on 2028-12-31, and only when rung 1 does not.\",\n      \"lo\": null,\n      \"hi\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"position\": 3,\n      \"label\": \"Still open\",\n      \"condition\": \"Neither rung 1 nor rung 2 holds on 2028-12-31.\",\n      \"lo\": null,\n      \"hi\": null\n    }\n  ],\n  \"reader_b_decisive_items\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"i100\",\n      \"day\": \"2026-07-05\",\n      \"title\": \"Nvidia's next-gen AI rack system delayed to 2028 on manufacturing snags, SemiAnalysis says\",\n      \"passage\": \"Kyber NVL144 rack architecture has been delayed to 2028 as the PCB midplane remains challenging from a manufacturability standpoint\",\n      \"meaning\": {\n        \"who\": \"SemiAnalysis (a research firm's report, not NVIDIA)\",\n        \"what\": \"Kyber NVL144 rack for Vera Rubin Ultra delayed; cause: PCB midplane manufacturability\",\n        \"how_much\": \"more than 12 months\",\n        \"period\": \"from 2027 to 2028\",\n        \"status\": \"claim\"\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"i179\",\n      \"day\": \"2026-07-06\",\n      \"title\": \"Nvidia's Kyber NVL144 reportedly pushed back more than a year, Asian suppliers drop\",\n      \"passage\": \"The more powerful version of the upcoming Rubin Ultra chip with four compute dies has also been canceled. Only the smaller two-die version remains, delivering roughly half the real-world compute performance.\",\n      \"meaning\": {\n        \"who\": \"SemiAnalysis\",\n        \"what\": \"four-die Rubin Ultra cancelled; two-die version remains\",\n        \"how_much\": \"roughly half the real-world compute\",\n        \"status\": \"claim\",\n        \"status_note\": \"cancelled\"\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"i181\",\n      \"day\": \"2026-07-06\",\n      \"title\": \"Nvidia's Kyber NVL144 reportedly pushed back more than a year, Asian suppliers drop\",\n      \"passage\": \"Nvidia plans to make up for the shortfall by selling more Oberon-Rubin racks in the existing form factor.\",\n      \"meaning\": {\n        \"who\": \"SemiAnalysis, about NVIDIA's plan\",\n        \"what\": \"sell more Oberon-Rubin racks to cover the Kyber gap\",\n        \"status\": \"plan\",\n        \"status_note\": \"as claimed\"\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"i101\",\n      \"day\": \"2026-07-05\",\n      \"title\": \"Nvidia's next-gen AI rack system delayed to 2028 on manufacturing snags, SemiAnalysis says\",\n      \"passage\": \"Our roadmap is intact\",\n      \"meaning\": {\n        \"who\": \"NVIDIA\",\n        \"what\": \"rejects the SemiAnalysis report; says its roadmap (Rubin Ultra / Kyber in 2027) is unchanged\",\n        \"status\": \"claim\"\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"i197\",\n      \"day\": \"2026-07-06\",\n      \"title\": \"Nvidia's Kyber rack for Rubin Ultra reportedly delayed to 2028, stopgap solution also axed due to customer pushback — Analyst firm SemiAnalysis says PCB midplane problems led to the delay [Updated]\",\n      \"passage\": \"An Nvidia representative responded with a short statement to Tom's Hardware : \\\"Our roadmap is intact.\\\"\",\n      \"meaning\": {\n        \"who\": \"NVIDIA representative\",\n        \"what\": \"denies the report: roadmap intact\",\n        \"condition\": \"unclear whether the original or an already updated roadmap is meant\",\n        \"status\": \"claim\"\n      }\n    }\n  ]\n}\nEVIDENCE COUNT 51\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json; p=json.load(open(\\\"/Users/lchingen/hivemind/clean-arch/e2e-comparison-2026-11-13/full-eligibility/controls/control-packet-6.json\\\"));\nfor x in p[1:]:\n print(\\\"CONTROL\\\",x[\\\"control\\\"]); seen=set();\n for e in x[\\\"evidence_shown_to_the_judge_through_2026_07_14\\\"]:\n  first=e.split(\\\"\\\\n\\\")[0]; key=first.split(\\\" [\\\")[0];\n  if key not in seen: print(first); seen.add(key)'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json; p=json.load(open(\\\"/Users/lchingen/hivemind/clean-arch/e2e-comparison-2026-11-13/full-eligibility/controls/control-packet-6.json\\\"));\nfor x in p[1:]:\n print(\\\"CONTROL\\\",x[\\\"control\\\"]); seen=set();\n for e in x[\\\"evidence_shown_to_the_judge_through_2026_07_14\\\"]:\n  first=e.split(\\\"\\\\n\\\")[0]; key=first.split(\\\" [\\\")[0];\n  if key not in seen: print(first); seen.add(key)'\"","aggregated_output":"CONTROL 22\n- e45 [fact; Noah Berman, The Wire (thewirechina.com); original source U.S. government (President Trump) ; from gnews:f8d63eab0337895e8e95cf02 2026-07-02] President Trump announced that his administration would allow NVIDIA to sell its H200 AI chips to China last December, after the U.S. government had restricted such sales under export controls the Biden administration put in place on the chip, which NVIDIA launched in 2023.\n- e46 [fact; Noah Berman, The Wire (thewirechina.com); original source Chinese government (as reported) ; from gnews:f8d63eab0337895e8e95cf02 2026-07-02] As of the 2026-07-02 article, the Chinese government had kept NVIDIA locked out of the China market despite the Trump administration having cleared the path for H200 exports.\n- e47 [party_statement; Noah Berman, The Wire (thewirechina.com); original source NVIDIA (CEO Jensen Huang) ; from gnews:f8d63eab0337895e8e95cf02 2026-07-02] NVIDIA CEO Jensen Huang has said NVIDIA's market share in China has fallen to zero since the Biden administration first imposed export controls on AI chips in 2022.\n- e153 [party_statement; Omor Ibne Ehsan, 247wallst.com; original source NVIDIA (Colette Kress, CFO) ; from gnews:4d1b1ca014068fb532f116b3 2026-07-06] NVIDIA's China data center compute revenue is effectively zero, and CFO Colette Kress said losing that market — which NVIDIA sizes at \"close to about $50 billion in the future\" — would be material.\n- e183 [third_party_view; Bloomberg (Business Times); original source Bloomberg Intelligence (survey report) 2026-07-07; from gnews:8d5c91ae833af14257a59b2b 2026-07-06] A Bloomberg Intelligence survey of 60 executives at Chinese software, finance, manufacturing and retail companies, released on Tuesday Jul 7, found respondents plan to allocate 46% of their AI accelerator budget to domestic products over the next 12 months, up from 30% today; the report said China's drive to substitute locally made AI semiconductors for foreign ones is making progress and is likely to benefit domestic makers such as Huawei and Hygon.\n- e184 [third_party_view; Bloomberg (Business Times); original source Bloomberg Intelligence (survey report) 2026-07-07; from gnews:8d5c91ae833af14257a59b2b 2026-07-06] The same Bloomberg Intelligence survey said Nvidia's products remain popular but its market share is expected to shrink as its H20 chips, which Beijing has urged Chinese tech firms not to use, become harder to find and local firms take over; Hygon Information Technology and Cambricon Technologies accelerators were being evaluated by a large pool of respondents, and Tencent, Alibaba and Huawei were named as best-positioned to gain from the shift.\n- e185 [fact; Bloomberg News; original source Chinese government ; from gnews:8d5c91ae833af14257a59b2b 2026-07-06] China is allocating roughly two trillion yuan (US$294 billion) to build data centres across the country over the next five years under a government-led initiative to expand AI use, and at least 80% of core technologies such as chips will be supplied by domestic companies, Bloomberg News reported.\n- e232 [third_party_view; idnfinancials.com; original source Richard Windsor, Radio Free Mobile ; from gnews:2957e5c79a365952140c8cce 2026-07-07] Analyst Richard Windsor of Radio Free Mobile said Nvidia's position in China is \"effectively at zero and will remain that way\" and that DeepSeek has almost no chance of selling chips outside China unless it gains access to advanced manufacturing technology, adding that DeepSeek's chip development would not affect Nvidia.\n- e236 [rumour; The Information, as reported by The Straits Times; original source The Information 2026-07-08; from gnews:c8c39f971721fd820aab5c1c 2026-07-08] The Information reported on 8 July 2026, citing two unidentified people familiar with the matter, that Chinese officials have told Alibaba Group, ByteDance and DeepSeek they will have permission to buy limited quantities of Nvidia's H200 AI chips, easing prior restrictions. Buyers must request approval specifying how many chips they need and why, and the Chinese government is still deciding the total allowed, which may end up fewer than 200,000 chips.\n- e238 [fact; The Straits Times; original source U.S. President Donald Trump ; from gnews:c8c39f971721fd820aab5c1c 2026-07-08] US President Donald Trump granted Nvidia permission in December to sell the processors (H200 AI chips) to China, but Chinese authorities have been slow to allow the technology into the country, the article reports.\n- e239 [party_statement; The Straits Times; original source Colette Kress, Nvidia CFO ; from gnews:c8c39f971721fd820aab5c1c 2026-07-08] In May, Nvidia chief financial officer Colette Kress said the H200 chips had yet to generate any revenue for the company in China, and that Nvidia was uncertain whether any imports would be allowed into the country.\n- e308 [party_statement; barchart.com; original source NVIDIA 2026-05-20; from gnews:57de52b4f33b0c903d256f9e 2026-07-09] Nvidia guided second-quarter fiscal 2027 revenue to $91 billion, plus or minus 2%, and a non-GAAP gross margin of about 75%, with the guidance assuming no contribution from China Data Center compute revenue because of ongoing U.S. export restrictions.\n- e315 [rumour; MIT Technology Review (The Download); original source The Information ; from gnews:50a84fa6b2f3eb33f29948a4 2026-07-09] The Information reported that China plans to let its top AI firms buy Nvidia's H200 AI chips, with Alibaba, ByteDance and DeepSeek set to receive permission; Reuters reported that China had previously withheld approval even though the U.S. had authorized the sales.\n- e316 [fact; South China Morning Post; original source U.S. government (Washington) ; from gnews:91684b863d274b10246d0c13 2026-07-09] In early 2026, the U.S. government (Washington) took the step of approving Nvidia's H200 graphics processing unit for export to China, according to the South China Morning Post.\n- e317 [rumour; South China Morning Post; original source Unnamed source with knowledge of the matter ; from gnews:91684b863d274b10246d0c13 2026-07-09] An unnamed source with knowledge of the matter told the South China Morning Post that China's government plans to let selected companies, including Alibaba Group Holding, buy limited numbers of Nvidia's H200 GPUs, as Beijing's stance begins to change.\n- e340 [fact; moomoo; original source China government ; from gnews:7b39859113d4e2b8ebd8aa86 2026-07-09] China is moving to grant limited approval for domestic firms to purchase Nvidia H200 processors, easing access to Nvidia's technology, though companies must now request individual approval for specific quantities and use cases.\n- e356 [party_statement; Vandita Jadeja (247wallst.com); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:f0423de52e9bd77b530ba76e 2026-07-10] NVIDIA's Q2 FY27 guidance explicitly excludes Data Center compute revenue from China, and the company reported zero H20 shipments in the quarter.\n- e362 [party_statement; David Moadel, 24/7 Wall St.; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:cbb9ff1a37ebb451f9321da5 2026-07-10] NVIDIA management guided Q2 FY2027 revenue to $91 billion, an outlook the company said assumed zero Data Center compute revenue from China; the article notes that any policy thaw on H200 exports could therefore be additional upside.\n- e427 [party_statement; barchart.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:ededac685b09f7a9c6952202 2026-07-12] Nvidia management guided second-quarter revenue to approximately $91 billion, despite assuming zero China data-center revenue because of export restrictions, signaling that the export restrictions remain binding on its China data-center business at the time of guidance.\n- e447 [party_statement; Alex Sirois, 247wallst.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:799ec454c15ddf61f08d042f 2026-07-13] The article states that NVIDIA's guidance for its Q2 assumes zero China Data Center compute revenue, a headwind it says Broadcom does not carry to the same degree.\n- e458 [fact; Vandita Jadeja (247wallst.com); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:b68c019528300c6a1fcd3515 2026-07-13] NVIDIA stated that no H20 compute products were shipped to China in Q1 FY27 and that its Q2 FY27 guidance excludes any Data Center compute revenue from China, indicating China data-center sales remain blocked.\n- e464 [fact; 24/7 Wall St. (Alex Sirois); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:221f52333c8f5e936e515c4d 2026-07-13] NVIDIA shipped zero H20 AI chips to China in the latest quarter, versus $4.6 billion of H20 revenue from China in the year-ago period, the article reports.\n- e470 [party_statement; CNBC; original source Jeffery Kessler, Under Secretary of Commerce for Industry and Security (U.S. Department of Commerce) 2026-07-14; from gnews:9d898f2bf43ed52a98e1587a 2026-07-14] At a congressional hearing on Tuesday (2026-07-14), Under Secretary of Commerce for Industry and Security Jeffery Kessler said \"very few\" of Nvidia's H200 AI chips have been shipped to China and Hong Kong, describing it as \"a very small quantity of chips\" and that \"very few shipments against licenses for H200s and equivalents have taken place.\" The article reports this as a sign H200 shipments to China have restarted. Nvidia declined to comment.\n- e471 [fact; CNBC; original source President Donald Trump / U.S. government ; from gnews:9d898f2bf43ed52a98e1587a 2026-07-14] In December, President Donald Trump said the U.S. government would approve China sales of the H200 AI chip in exchange for a 25% cut, and licenses for the chips were issued earlier this year (2026); some in the administration say the chips can be used for military purposes. This describes the U.S. approval policy for H200 China sales currently in force.\n- e472 [party_statement; CNBC; original source Jeffery Kessler, Under Secretary of Commerce for Industry and Security (U.S. Department of Commerce) 2026-07-14; from gnews:9d898f2bf43ed52a98e1587a 2026-07-14] Kessler said the U.S. government assesses companies that want H200 chips on a case-by-case basis, with applicants needing to meet national security requirements and submit to inspections to ensure the chips are compliant, and that \"there are cases where we deny the license applications we receive.\"\n- e473 [party_statement; CNBC; original source Nvidia / CEO Jensen Huang ; from gnews:9d898f2bf43ed52a98e1587a 2026-07-14] Since last year Nvidia has excluded any potential Chinese AI chip revenue from its forecasts, and CEO Jensen Huang said on CNBC in May that he told investors to \"expect nothing\" from Chinese sales. Nvidia's guidance therefore assumes no China AI chip revenue.\n- e484 [party_statement; Yahoo Finance (finance.yahoo.com); original source U.S. commerce official ; from gnews:c73b90b63a733c6ab712fcdd 2026-07-14] A top U.S. commerce official told Congress that Nvidia has begun shipping its H200 AI chips to China, though the number shipped so far is 'very few'; the report cites this confirmation, alongside Morgan Stanley's reiterated 'overweight', as a reason Nvidia shares jumped 4% in the afternoon session on 2026-07-14.\n- e494 [party_statement; Vandita Jadeja (247wallst.com); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:1e1fa5a194198eaf05d90c9e 2026-07-14] NVIDIA's guidance excludes any Data Center compute revenue from China, which the article calls a real overhang.\n- e500 [fact; Jowi Morales, Tom's Hardware, citing the Financial Times; original source U.S. government (President Donald Trump) ; from gnews:2a41a34c16f5280b624481fb 2026-07-14] President Donald Trump reversed course in December 2025 and allowed Nvidia to export its H200 GPUs to select customers in the region, per the article; it says this would have alleviated the Chinese supply situation had Beijing not blocked Chinese companies from buying the processors.\n- e508 [party_statement; Vandita Jadeja, 247wallst.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:184645c8d683d95b684bd6da 2026-07-14] NVIDIA guided Q2 revenue to $91 billion, a forecast management said assumes zero China Data Center compute revenue.\n- e517 [rumour; Reuters; original source U.S. government (Commerce Department/Bureau of Industry and Security) ; from gnews:24268bfaab6ef1d7876b188c 2026-07-14] U.S. authorities have granted approval to ZTE Kangxun Telecom and server maker Maginfra to purchase Nvidia's H200 AI chips, and cleared Zhuhai Hengqin Yunxiang Zhisheng Network Technology, a Kingsoft subsidiary, to use some AMD chips that rival the H200, according to documents and two unnamed sources. The three firms had not previously been reported to have received U.S. clearance, expanding the known set of licensed buyers beyond China's largest internet groups and major electronics distributors. ZTE, Maginfra, Kingsoft, Nvidia, AMD and China's Ministry of Commerce did not respond to requests for comment; BIS did not immediately reply.\n- e518 [rumour; Reuters; original source Chinese cloud computing firms (unnamed) / Chinese authorities on import review ; from gnews:24268bfaab6ef1d7876b188c 2026-07-14] Some Chinese cloud firms have recently told partners and clients they may soon be able to obtain Nvidia's H200 chips, according to the two unnamed sources, which Reuters said indicates some progress in the import reviews by Chinese authorities.\n- e525 [fact; Reuters (relayed by Tom's Hardware / Bruno Ferreira); original source U.S. government ; from gnews:3221eba2826b001b12b82770 2026-07-14] Reuters reported that Chinese telecoms giant ZTE and server firm Maginfra received U.S. government approval to buy Nvidia's last-generation H200 'Hopper' AI chips, joining Alibaba, Tencent, ByteDance and JD.com among roughly 10 Chinese companies with U.S. clearance for such purchases.\n- e527 [third_party_view; Reuters (relayed by Tom's Hardware / Bruno Ferreira); original source Chinese government (stance/approval status), per Reuters ; from gnews:3221eba2826b001b12b82770 2026-07-14] Reuters reported that there was no word on whether Chinese authorities would give ZTE the go-ahead to import the H200 chips, noting China has taken a protectionist stance, discouraging firms from buying foreign tech and pushing them toward homegrown accelerators such as Huawei's.\n- e528 [third_party_view; Reuters (relayed by Tom's Hardware / Bruno Ferreira); original source Reuters (reported Chinese order book) ; from gnews:3221eba2826b001b12b82770 2026-07-14] Reuters reported, around six months before the 2026-07-14 article, that Chinese tech firms had more than two million H200 chips on order, far more than Nvidia had on hand at the time.\n- e529 [fact; Tom's Hardware (Bruno Ferreira); original source U.S. government ; from gnews:3221eba2826b001b12b82770 2026-07-14] The item describes the standing U.S. export-control framework: U.S. authorities allow Chinese firms to buy AI chips up to and including the Hopper family (meaning no Blackwell chips), with a 25% export tariff, with final decisions made case-by-case.\n- e530 [party_statement; CNBC (relayed by Tom's Hardware); original source U.S. trade official ; from gnews:3221eba2826b001b12b82770 2026-07-14] A U.S. trade official, cited by CNBC at a congressional hearing, stated that 'very few shipments against licenses for H200s and equivalents have taken place. It's a very small quantity of chips.'\nCONTROL 23\n- e82 [party_statement; fool.com (Motley Fool) article author; original source The big four AI hyperscalers ; from gnews:92c7e54d38dc2916aa185568 2026-07-04] The article reports that the big four AI hyperscalers plan to spend around $650 billion on data center capital expenditures in the current year (2026). This is a planned spending level by the customer group, not a committed order for NVIDIA.\n- e83 [party_statement; fool.com (Motley Fool) article author; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:92c7e54d38dc2916aa185568 2026-07-04] NVIDIA projects that the big four AI hyperscalers' combined data center capital expenditures could reach $1 trillion next year (2027), up from the roughly $650 billion planned for 2026. This is NVIDIA's own projection, not a reported outcome.\n- e55 [fact; Tom's Hardware; original source  ; from gnews:b680fbe2c3e9228eba33a0b4 2026-07-02] Tom's Hardware reported that public opposition to data centers — blamed for large increases in power/utility costs and water consumption, with 7 in 10 Americans saying they do not want a data center in their backyard — led to the delay or cancellation of at least 75 data center projects in the first quarter of 2026, a demand signal for AI infrastructure.\n- e125 [party_statement; Keithen Drury (fool.com); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:71df54965281684cc500960f 2026-07-06] On its most recent quarterly conference call, NVIDIA management forecast that hyperscaler spending would top $1 trillion in 2027, after reaching a projected $650 billion in 2026.\n- e158 [third_party_view; Allen Harris, The Berkshire Edge; original source Goldman Sachs ; from gnews:54aa9ce326115cedf8857512 2026-07-06] Goldman Sachs estimates annual AI infrastructure capital expenditures could reach $765 billion in 2026 and grow to $1.6 trillion by 2031, for cumulative spending of about $7.6 trillion from 2026 through 2031. The spending spans chips, data centers, power, cooling and networking required to run AI at scale.\n- e161 [third_party_view; autonocion.com, citing Tom's Hardware; original source Tom's Hardware ; from gnews:417f6244f0aaa27123023deb 2026-07-06] More than 75 data center projects worth around $130 billion have been blocked or delayed across the US so far this year, according to a running tally at Tom's Hardware, and polling cited in the same reporting found 7 in 10 Americans oppose a data center built near their home.\n- e170 [party_statement; Mizuho analyst Jordan Klein, reported by Investing.com (uk.finance.yahoo.com); original source Hon Hai Precision Industry Co Ltd (management) ; from gnews:d5c48abb470f394f86549b6b 2026-07-06] Hon Hai Precision Industry, one of Nvidia's largest suppliers, reported strong earnings on the Friday before the 2026-07-06 article and its management forecast a continued acceleration in AI demand; Mizuho analyst Jordan Klein called this \"a very good sign into the NVDA launch of their new Vera Rubin rack.\" No figures for the results were given.\n- e196 [third_party_view; fool.com; original source fool.com author (aggregating the big four AI hyperscalers' capex plans) ; from gnews:f8191f5bb960d79e47c73d6c 2026-07-07] The article states that in calendar 2026 the big four AI hyperscalers plan to spend around $650 billion on data center capital expenditures.\n- e205 [third_party_view; Keithen Drury, fool.com; original source AI hyperscalers (capex plans; 2027 forecast is the author's) ; from gnews:d0f3f83296df884fae64921e 2026-07-07] Fool.com's Keithen Drury writes that the AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year (calendar 2026), and that next year (calendar 2027) that figure could be over $1 trillion.\n- e215 [fact; 24/7 Wall St.; original source Google ; from gnews:295fceb25273abae9bb6c995 2026-07-07] Google reported that Google Cloud revenue rose 63% with backlog nearly doubling to over $460 billion, and guided 2026 capital expenditure up to $190 billion.\n- e250 [fact; Bloomberg (reported by Futuriom); original source Meta 2026-07-01; from gnews:cb05b9f49943ef0ba412cf36 2026-07-08] Bloomberg reported (July 1) that Meta is setting up a cloud-based business selling AI compute and models, on the same day NVIDIA announced its new revenue-sharing model; the article notes Meta had planned to buy 'millions' of GPUs from NVIDIA earlier this year and questions what its move implies for AI demand.\n- e256 [fact; 247wallst.com (Gerelyn Terzo); original source IES Holdings ; from gnews:abfc64ce8fc333f13d06a757 2026-07-08] IES Holdings, a data-center electrical and technology systems contractor, reported March-quarter 2026 results: Communications segment revenue rose 35% year over year to $367.7 million, which it attributed to continued strong demand in the data center market; Infrastructure Solutions revenue jumped 64% to $192.4 million; and total backlog rose 62% since the end of fiscal 2025 to $3.86 billion, driven by customer demand and expansion capacity.\n- e257 [fact; 247wallst.com (Gerelyn Terzo); original source Quanta Services ; from gnews:abfc64ce8fc333f13d06a757 2026-07-08] Quanta Services, a grid and electrical infrastructure contractor, reported Q1 2026 revenue of $7.87 billion, up 26.3% year over year, adjusted EPS of $2.68 versus $2.03 consensus, and backlog that rose to a record $48.5 billion; management raised full-year 2026 guidance to $34.70–35.20 billion in revenue and $13.55–14.25 in adjusted EPS, and cited a path to more than double adjusted EPS by 2030.\n- e258 [fact; 247wallst.com (Gerelyn Terzo); original source Comfort Systems USA ; from gnews:abfc64ce8fc333f13d06a757 2026-07-08] Comfort Systems USA, a mechanical/HVAC contractor, reported Q1 2026 revenue of $2.87 billion, up 56.5% year over year (51% organic), diluted EPS of $10.51 versus $6.81 consensus, and backlog of $12.45 billion, nearly double the $6.89 billion a year earlier; data center and technology infrastructure now accounts for roughly 45% of company revenue.\n- e259 [party_statement; 247wallst.com (Gerelyn Terzo); original source Comfort Systems USA (CFO Bill George) ; from gnews:abfc64ce8fc333f13d06a757 2026-07-08] On the Q1 2026 call, Comfort Systems USA finance chief Bill George said the constraint on the business is now a supply (labor) issue rather than demand, saying 'There is plenty more work we could take if we could possibly do it' — indicating data-center work demand exceeds the contractor's capacity to execute.\n- e260 [fact; 247wallst.com (Gerelyn Terzo); original source EMCOR Group ; from gnews:abfc64ce8fc333f13d06a757 2026-07-08] EMCOR Group reported Q1 2026 diluted EPS of $6.84 versus $5.90 consensus, Remaining Performance Obligations at a record $15.62 billion, up 32.9% year over year, and raised full-year 2026 guidance to $18.50–19.25 billion in revenue with EPS of $28.25–29.75.\n- e261 [fact; 247wallst.com (Gerelyn Terzo); original source Sterling Infrastructure ; from gnews:abfc64ce8fc333f13d06a757 2026-07-08] Sterling Infrastructure reported Q1 2026 EPS of $3.59 (beating consensus by a double-digit percentage), revenue up 91.59% to $825.7 million, and E-Infrastructure Solutions revenue up 174% year over year to $597.7 million at a 23.5% adjusted operating margin; it raised full-year guidance to adjusted diluted EPS of $18.40–19.05, implying 72% growth at the midpoint.\n- e353 [fact; Reuters (reported by gurufocus.com); original source Meta Platforms ; from gnews:09b9d76297a57f198651c5e7 2026-07-10] Reuters reported that Meta Platforms plans to begin manufacturing its custom AI processor, code-named Iris, in September after completing initial testing. The chip is being designed with Broadcom and manufactured by TSMC, and the report said it is intended to support Meta's AI infrastructure while complementing, rather than replacing, the large number of graphics processors Meta continues to buy from Nvidia and Advanced Micro Devices.\n- e445 [fact; Alex Sirois, 247wallst.com; original source Broadcom ; from gnews:799ec454c15ddf61f08d042f 2026-07-13] Broadcom reported Q2 FY2026 revenue of $22.187 billion, up 47.9%, including AI semiconductor sales of $10.80 billion that grew 143%, as reported by 247wallst.com analyst Alex Sirois.\n- e449 [party_statement; Alex Sirois, 247wallst.com; original source Broadcom ; from gnews:799ec454c15ddf61f08d042f 2026-07-13] Broadcom management has telegraphed $16.0 billion of AI revenue for its Q3, roughly 54% of total sales, ahead of its Q3 report scheduled for September 8, according to the article.\n- e467 [party_statement; 24/7 Wall St. (Alex Sirois); original source Meta Platforms ; from gnews:221f52333c8f5e936e515c4d 2026-07-13] Meta Platforms raised its 2026 capital expenditure plan to $125-$145 billion, the article reports.\n- e475 [party_statement; Justin Pope, The Motley Fool; original source Meta Platforms, Microsoft, Alphabet and Amazon (the big four hyperscalers) ; from gnews:84e90d3da2d3779da0e7bdb0 2026-07-14] The article reports that the big four AI hyperscalers — Meta Platforms, Microsoft, Alphabet and Amazon — are planning higher capital expenditures in 2026. No individual capex figures are given. Reported by Justin Pope of The Motley Fool.\n- e476 [third_party_view; Justin Pope, The Motley Fool; original source Goldman Sachs ; from gnews:84e90d3da2d3779da0e7bdb0 2026-07-14] Goldman Sachs estimates that industry AI compute spending will grow from approximately $494 billion in 2026 to $1.13 trillion by 2031, as reported by The Motley Fool's Justin Pope. This is a broader industry demand forecast, not a figure for NVIDIA or for the big four's data-center capex specifically.\n- e496 [party_statement; Vandita Jadeja (247wallst.com); original source Meta ; from gnews:1e1fa5a194198eaf05d90c9e 2026-07-14] Meta's $145 billion infrastructure budget, aimed partly at custom silicon, was cited among competitive threats facing NVIDIA.\n- e514 [party_statement; 247wallst.com (Vandita Jadeja); original source Broadcom (Hock Tan, CEO) ; from gnews:386a294bd395bde25e3f4a11 2026-07-14] Broadcom CEO Hock Tan said momentum continues, with fiscal Q3 AI semiconductor revenue set to grow more than 200% year over year to $16 billion; Broadcom guided total Q3 revenue to $29.4 billion (+84% YoY), set a stated goal of exceeding $100 billion in AI sales by 2027, and reported AI semiconductor revenue of $10.8 billion last quarter, up 143% YoY — custom AI silicon revenue that competes for the same AI data-center spending as NVIDIA, per 247wallst.com's 2026-07-14 article.\n- e535 [fact; EBC (ebc.com); original source Amazon.com Inc. ; from gnews:f83ae0a4f01c18d48f20a629 2026-07-14] Amazon recently borrowed another $25 billion, causing a minor selloff in AI-related debt, as investors question hyperscaler returns on their large AI hardware spending, EBC reported.\nCONTROL 24\n- e90 [third_party_view; CNBC; original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:552f8c2bdfd080555d592587 2026-07-05] Research firm SemiAnalysis reported that NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the server cabinet designed to pack 144 GPUs and slated to debut with NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin Ultra system in 2027 — has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028, because the multi-layer PCB midplane at the heart of the system 'remains challenging from a manufacturability standpoint.' SemiAnalysis also said the larger NVL576 system, which links eight racks via optical connections, is likely delayed or limited to small volumes.\n- e91 [third_party_view; CNBC; original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:552f8c2bdfd080555d592587 2026-07-05] SemiAnalysis reported that NVIDIA's backup plan — bolting two of its current-generation racks together to achieve similar power — has been scrapped after cloud service providers and hyperscalers pushed back on the design's 'odd design and heavy operational burden.' SemiAnalysis said this leaves NVIDIA with 'no proven solution to expand the scale-up world size for Rubin Ultra,' which it predicted could give AMD and Google a rare technical opening at the high end of the market.\n- e92 [party_statement; CNBC; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:552f8c2bdfd080555d592587 2026-07-05] NVIDIA rejected the SemiAnalysis report that its Kyber/Rubin Ultra roadmap had slipped, stating 'Our roadmap is intact.'\n- e106 [third_party_view; Lockridge Okoth, beincrypto.com (via finance.yahoo.com); original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:68a3ed21f77b935d9fa7ec5e 2026-07-06] Research firm SemiAnalysis claimed that NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 high-density rack architecture, built for Rubin Ultra GPUs and showcased at GTC earlier this year, has slipped by more than 12 months, blaming persistent manufacturing problems with the system's complex PCB midplane.\n- e108 [party_statement; Lockridge Okoth, beincrypto.com (via finance.yahoo.com); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:68a3ed21f77b935d9fa7ec5e 2026-07-06] NVIDIA rejected SemiAnalysis's delay claims, telling media outlets that its roadmap remains intact.\n- e122 [third_party_view; DataCenterDynamics (Charlotte Trueman), reporting SemiAnalysis's claims; original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:98a4e8ff0c0260d8dc9e531d 2026-07-06] SemiAnalysis reported (in an item published 2026-07-06) that Nvidia has delayed production of its Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture by more than 12 months, pushing its release to 2028 from an original 2027 target, because of manufacturing challenges with the printed circuit board (PCB) midplane that connects the modules. SemiAnalysis also said Nvidia's alternative plan — placing two Oberon racks back-to-back to achieve similar power output — was scrapped after cloud companies and hyperscalers pushed back, and that the same manufacturing issues will likely also delay the planned NVL576 system or limit it to small volumes.\n- e123 [third_party_view; DataCenterDynamics (Charlotte Trueman), reporting SemiAnalysis's claims; original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:98a4e8ff0c0260d8dc9e531d 2026-07-06] SemiAnalysis separately reported, about a week before the 2026-07-06 DCD article, that Nvidia had canceled the four-die Rubin Ultra chip design in favor of a two-chiplet design, citing manufacturing execution concerns; per SemiAnalysis, the two-die offering will deliver around half the performance of the four-die Rubin Ultra.\n- e124 [party_statement; DataCenterDynamics (Charlotte Trueman); original source Nvidia 2026-07-06; from gnews:98a4e8ff0c0260d8dc9e531d 2026-07-06] In response to a DataCenterDynamics request for comment on SemiAnalysis's Kyber claims, an Nvidia spokesperson said: \"Our roadmap is intact.\"\n- e134 [rumour; 247wallst.com (Danielle Liverance); original source  ; from gnews:3df0bbc8aff2c1d008bc0a32 2026-07-06] NVIDIA shares were reported to be under pressure on reports of delays across its upcoming lineup of Vera Rubin server systems; the reports are unattributed in the article.\n- e164 [third_party_view; the-decoder.com (reporting SemiAnalysis's post on X); original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:795ea94e1100415a67fd1a96 2026-07-06] Analyst firm SemiAnalysis reported on X that Nvidia's next-generation AI server rack system, Kyber NVL144, has been pushed back more than twelve months to 2028 because of manufacturing problems, specifically the PCB midplane — a central circuit board connecting all the individual components that has proven extremely difficult to produce without defects.\n- e165 [third_party_view; the-decoder.com (reporting SemiAnalysis); original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:795ea94e1100415a67fd1a96 2026-07-06] SemiAnalysis reported additional setbacks to Nvidia's roadmap: a planned alternative design, NVL72x2 — which would have placed two Oberon racks back to back — has been scrapped entirely after cloud providers and large data-center operators pushed back on its unusual form factor and high operational overhead; and the more powerful version of the upcoming Rubin Ultra chip with four compute dies has been canceled, leaving only the smaller two-die version that delivers roughly half the real-world compute performance.\n- e166 [third_party_view; the-decoder.com (reporting SemiAnalysis); original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:795ea94e1100415a67fd1a96 2026-07-06] SemiAnalysis reported that Nvidia's key interconnect technology CPO-NVSwitch, which links many chips into a single large system, will not arrive until the generation after next, called Feynman, meaning Nvidia lacks a proven way to scale Rubin Ultra to very large systems for now; the report says the gap could give competitors like AMD's MI500X or Google's TPUv8i Broadfly room to move in, and Nvidia plans to make up for the shortfall by selling more Oberon-Rubin racks in the existing form factor.\n- e167 [rumour; Investing.com (uk.finance.yahoo.com); original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:d5c48abb470f394f86549b6b 2026-07-06] Supply-chain research firm SemiAnalysis claimed in a weekend report (reported 2026-07-06) that Nvidia's Kyber NVL144 rack architecture will be delayed to 2028 because of manufacturing challenges with its PCB midplane, a claim Nvidia disputed.\n- e168 [party_statement; NVIDIA spokesperson, via Investing.com (uk.finance.yahoo.com); original source NVIDIA Corporation ; from gnews:d5c48abb470f394f86549b6b 2026-07-06] An Nvidia spokesperson, in a brief statement emailed to Investing.com, refuted SemiAnalysis's claims that its next-generation rack architectures are delayed, saying: \"Our roadmap is intact.\"\n- e169 [rumour; Investing.com (uk.finance.yahoo.com); original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:d5c48abb470f394f86549b6b 2026-07-06] SemiAnalysis's report also alleged that Nvidia cancelled an alternative back-to-back rack design after heavy operational pushback from hyperscale cloud clients, and that a crucial four-die configuration of a downsized version of the upcoming Rubin Ultra platform had been scrapped.\n- e171 [third_party_view; tikr.com (TIKR); original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:d4c5dc540392f8df39aee250 2026-07-06] Research firm SemiAnalysis reported that Nvidia's next rack-scale server design, Kyber — built to pack 144 Nvidia chips into a single cabinet — has been pushed back more than a year, from 2027 to 2028, due to manufacturing problems with a specialized circuit board. SemiAnalysis also reported that the larger NVL576 version (eight racks linked optically) is likely to be delayed or ship only in small volumes, and that Nvidia's backup plan of bolting two current-generation racks together was scrapped after cloud customers reportedly rejected it as awkward and expensive to run. SemiAnalysis said this leaves Nvidia without a proven way to scale up Rubin Ultra for now, potentially giving rivals like AMD and Google an opening at the high end of the AI chip market.\n- e175 [third_party_view; Bloomberg / businesstimes.com.sg; original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:c0432377817a1c00a1bbadf0 2026-07-06] Research firm SemiAnalysis said in an X post that Nvidia's next-generation Kyber NVL144 AI server rack system has been delayed by more than a year due to manufacturing difficulties, specifically setbacks in the construction of printed circuit boards for the platform. Nvidia did not respond to a request for comment. The report was unconfirmed by Nvidia.\n- e177 [rumour; Tom's Hardware (Luke James), reporting a SemiAnalysis thread on X; original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:c75f16a6d64cc27fc28fb571 2026-07-06] According to SemiAnalysis, in a thread on X reported by Tom's Hardware, NVIDIA will not ship its Kyber NVL144 rack until 2028 — a delay of more than 12 months — pushing the cabinet intended for 2027's Rubin Ultra GPUs into the following year. The report attributes the holdup to manufacturing challenges with the PCB midplane (which NVIDIA calls the orthogonal backplane) that connects eight Oberon racks between the NVSwitches.\n- e178 [rumour; Tom's Hardware (Luke James), reporting SemiAnalysis; original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:c75f16a6d64cc27fc28fb571 2026-07-06] SemiAnalysis reported that NVIDIA killed NVL72x2, a stopgap rack that would have bolted two Oberon racks back-to-back to reach Kyber-class density over copper NVLink, after its largest customers balked at running two linked cabinets as a single unit. The report says no proven alternative remains to widen Rubin Ultra's scale-up in 2027.\n- e179 [third_party_view; Tom's Hardware (Luke James), reporting SemiAnalysis; original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:c75f16a6d64cc27fc28fb571 2026-07-06] SemiAnalysis said NVL576 — a separate configuration tying eight racks together through co-packaged optics — is likely to slip too, or to ship in low volume, until that optical technology matures.\n- e180 [party_statement; Tom's Hardware (Luke James); original source NVIDIA 2026-07-06; from gnews:c75f16a6d64cc27fc28fb571 2026-07-06] An NVIDIA representative told Tom's Hardware, in a statement dated 7/6/2026, that \"Our roadmap is intact,\" providing no further details in response to follow-up questions about the reported Kyber delay.\n- e181 [rumour; Tom's Hardware (Luke James), reporting SemiAnalysis; original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:c75f16a6d64cc27fc28fb571 2026-07-06] According to the report, NVIDIA dropped the quad-chiplet Rubin Ultra GPU for a dual-chiplet part about a week before the 7/6/2026 article, over manufacturing execution concerns, halving the accelerator's per-package compute.\n- e182 [third_party_view; Tom's Hardware (Luke James); original source  ; from gnews:c75f16a6d64cc27fc28fb571 2026-07-06] Tom's Hardware reported that the delay applies only to the Rubin Ultra phase and its Kyber rack; NVIDIA's 2026 Rubin GPUs, which reuse the current Oberon rack, are not part of the reported delay.\n- e194 [third_party_view; CNBC; original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:b48593184704bbbe6eddbc03 2026-07-07] A research report, identified later in the CNBC article as a SemiAnalysis report, claimed that Nvidia was at least a year behind on manufacturing of its next line of server racks (the article refers to the next-gen Kyber server rack running into delays). CNBC reported the claim as the backdrop to Nvidia's share performance a day after the report; the item gives no specific dates, volumes or shipment figures for the delay itself.\n- e195 [party_statement; CNBC; original source NVIDIA 2026-07-06; from gnews:b48593184704bbbe6eddbc03 2026-07-07] Nvidia disputed a SemiAnalysis report that its next-gen Kyber server rack was running into delays, according to CNBC; the item reports the dispute occurred on the Monday before the 2026-07-07 article and does not quote Nvidia's statement or give any revised schedule. Nvidia's denial is the company's own assertion that its product plan/schedule stands.\n- e210 [third_party_view; Danielle Liverance, 24/7 Wall St. (citing CNBC and the SemiAnalysis post); original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:295fceb25273abae9bb6c995 2026-07-07] Semiconductor research firm SemiAnalysis reported on July 5–6, 2026 (cited by CNBC) that NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale AI system has been delayed from a planned 2027 launch to 2028, with the 78-layer PCB midplane named as the manufacturability bottleneck, and said the larger NVL576 (linking eight racks via optical connections) is also likely delayed or limited to small initial volumes.\n- e211 [party_statement; 24/7 Wall St. (citing Bloomberg); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:295fceb25273abae9bb6c995 2026-07-07] NVIDIA publicly disputed SemiAnalysis's delay report on Monday, with a spokesperson telling Bloomberg that its 'roadmap is intact.'\n- e251 [third_party_view; SemiAnalysis (X post), reported by Futuriom; original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:cb05b9f49943ef0ba412cf36 2026-07-08] Research firm SemiAnalysis stated in an X post that NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack system has hit manufacturing setbacks; the article says this could push NVIDIA to use its new credit/revenue-sharing model to sustain demand.\n- e252 [party_statement; NVIDIA spokesperson; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:cb05b9f49943ef0ba412cf36 2026-07-08] An NVIDIA spokesperson said 'Our road map is intact' and that 'Vera Rubin Ultra will scale up to a larger NVL576 domain in its planned 2027 timeline,' responding to reported Kyber NVL144 setbacks.\n- e305 [rumour; barchart.com; original source  ; from gnews:57de52b4f33b0c903d256f9e 2026-07-09] Unnamed reports suggested that Nvidia's next-generation Kyber rack-scale AI infrastructure could be delayed until 2028, against a prior expectation that it would launch in 2027.\n- e306 [party_statement; barchart.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:57de52b4f33b0c903d256f9e 2026-07-09] Nvidia denied speculation that its Kyber rack-scale AI infrastructure is delayed, maintaining that its roadmap remains on track; the article states Kyber, designed for the Rubin Ultra platform, is expected to launch in 2027 as an integrated AI infrastructure system.\n- e319 [party_statement; IEEE Spectrum (Knvul Sheikh); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:a4eb2bdfabca94f5887881c6 2026-07-09] Nvidia plans to add even more processors to single interconnected systems, going from 72 GPUs today to as many as 576 by 2027, as it scales its rack-scale systems, according to IEEE Spectrum.\n- e339 [party_statement; Bloomberg (via moomoo); original source Nvidia ; from gnews:7b39859113d4e2b8ebd8aa86 2026-07-09] Nvidia issued a statement to Bloomberg denying claims of a technical delay and confirming that its product roadmap remains intact, a clarification the article says counters concerns that infrastructure bottlenecks could disrupt its AI competitive lead.\n- e389 [party_statement; finance.yahoo.com; original source NVIDIA Corp. ; from gnews:1c3c0c58ed5ec3da43292f9b 2026-07-11] NVIDIA Corp. has denied reports that its Kyber platform — tied to its next-generation AI chips — is being delayed, according to a July 11, 2026 article that frames the denial against reports of a delay to its next generation AI chips.\n- e423 [third_party_view; Geoffrey Seiler, fool.com; original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:3cd991655d4cdeaa4a578b84 2026-07-12] SemiAnalysis reported that Nvidia's Kyber NVL144 next-generation AI rack system could be delayed until 2028 because of problems manufacturing a crucial circuit board.\n- e424 [party_statement; Geoffrey Seiler, fool.com; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:3cd991655d4cdeaa4a578b84 2026-07-12] Nvidia said its roadmap remains intact, responding to the SemiAnalysis report that the Kyber NVL144 next-generation AI rack system could be delayed until 2028, though the article says questions linger.\n- e434 [party_statement; gurufocus.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:dedacecd6d2ed5192c307199 2026-07-13] In investor-relations discussions, NVIDIA said development of its next-generation Rubin Ultra platform remains on schedule and confirmed there have been no changes to its NVLink architecture.\n- e532 [third_party_view; EBC (ebc.com); original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:f83ae0a4f01c18d48f20a629 2026-07-14] Industry research firm SemiAnalysis reported over the weekend before the 2026-07-14 article that Nvidia's upcoming Kyber NVL144 architecture could face delays extending into 2028.\n- e533 [party_statement; EBC (ebc.com); original source NVIDIA Corp. (spokesperson) ; from gnews:f83ae0a4f01c18d48f20a629 2026-07-14] An Nvidia spokesperson countered SemiAnalysis's report on a Kyber NVL144 delay, saying the company's \"roadmap is intact.\"\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
